And he said something about it.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:04.140
And because of that, he himself was put through hell.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:07.540
So this interview, I think you guys are going to love it.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:11.980
Truly, by the end of it, it's like, I almost, you almost want to cry at the end.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:17.460
Because we do see just how pervasive the intelligence community is.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:26.260
And just sort of how they manipulate and control our everyday lives is something that we always need to be aware of.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:28.500
And I think John does a great job of shedding light on that.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:29.820
Yes, it's a very sad story.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.820
But as you pointed out, a very heroic story.

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:34.300
John was in the CIA for a long time.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:35.780
And yes, he was a whistleblower.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:42.700
He exposed the Bush administration's torture programs that were codified and known about throughout the administration.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.980
The interesting thing, of course, like you mentioned, the strong arm of the intelligence agencies.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:54.740
John didn't get convicted or even had charges brought against him until the Obama administration came and took care of that.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.180
So his story is fascinating.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:58.300
Again, Republicans, Democrats, it doesn't matter.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:05.260
The intelligence agencies, if you try to turn against them and do the right thing as John tried to do, they will turn against you.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:09.500
So we see that until this day, it still permeates throughout our culture.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:10.300
Absolutely.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:13.180
And John is also the author of a series of books.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:18.860
One being Doing Time Like a Spy, another one, The CIA Guide to Surveillance.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:23.140
And of course, another one, The CIA Guide to Disappearing,

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:25.460
which is about as scary as it gets.

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:28.060
And of course, his first book was The Reluctant Spy.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:32.580
And you get to hear all about his story from there, or you get to hear all about it from him today.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.020
Absolutely. Fernando?

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:37.860
I just, I cannot wait for people to finish this interview.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:38.300
Awesome.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:39.020
It was intense.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:41.060
All right, let's just hop right into it.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:43.380
Please enjoy this interview.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:44.220
All right, everyone.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:49.540
Now it is time for our interview and conversation with a man that Travis Irvine brought to our attention.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:53.020
Travis, would you like to tell us a little bit about John Carriacou?

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.940
Oh, yes. Well, John Carriacou is an American hero.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:00.540
He is a former intelligence officer with the US intelligence services.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:02.300
He served this country well.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:06.660
And of course, he's got an incredible story about being a whistleblower in this country

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:09.740
and what that is like for those types of people.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:15.620
Of course, John was on tour with us on the Liberty Tour 2016 with Gary Johnson.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:16.940
And that is how I know him.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:18.980
Ben, you also spoke at a number of our rallies.

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:24.460
Technically, we are all Liberty Lovin' Liberal Lovers.

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:26.660
Yes. Perfect, Travis.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:33.380
All right, everyone, a good whistleblower, unlike those NFL referees, which nobody likes.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:36.500
But John Carriacou, thank you so much for being on the show.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:37.660
My pleasure.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:39.020
So let's just start things off.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:41.300
Obviously, we've been talking a little bit about General Milley.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:45.420
We're constantly talking about the surveillance state, the extension of the surveillance state.

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:48.020
It seems as if they're turning the cameras inward.

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:51.460
And now we are the specimen that they are hunting.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:55.860
So can you tell us a little bit about your background as a whistleblower?

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:57.780
How did you get to this position?

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:03.820
And I can't imagine as a child, you think, I, when I grow up, I want to be a whistleblower.

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:06.260
It's something that just kind of happens.

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:07.420
So just happens.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:08.940
Yeah, can you tell us your experience?

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:14.620
Yeah, no child in his or her right mind would ever want to aspire to be a whistleblower.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:17.420
Nothing good comes of it other than the fact

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:20.380
that you can sleep at night and your children respect you.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:24.060
So, you know, at least at least you have that as a good thing.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:28.260
What led you down the path of being a truth teller?

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:30.980
What was happening where you said, I can't, I'm not sleeping.

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:34.020
If I know this information and I know there's deception going on.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:35.700
What what was it for you?

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:37.780
It was a combination of things.

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:42.020
You know, on my very first day of federal service, I stood in the CIA's

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:46.500
auditorium with 300 other new hires on our very first day at work.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:50.820
And I put my right hand in the air and I swore to protect and to uphold

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:54.940
the Constitution against all enemies, foreign and domestic.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:59.140
And, you know, in retrospect, I hate to even think

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:01.620
that I was the only person in the room that day that meant it.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:06.180
I've always been I've always been a person

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:09.260
who was who was respectful of the rule of law.

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:15.300
Even if I didn't agree with the rule or the law, you know, we're Americans.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:17.580
And there's a way to go about changing laws.

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:23.460
And so you can't just decide you don't like a law and then pretend that it doesn't exist.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:27.420
Right. And after 14 years, 15 years at the CIA,

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:31.740
the torture program began.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:37.540
And to me, this was it was obviously unethical and immoral,

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:39.620
but it was also very clearly illegal.

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:43.060
And I always thought, well, you know, I'm anti torture,

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:45.700
but reasonable people can agree to disagree.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:48.740
But by God, if you want to torture people in this country,

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:52.420
you've got to change the law and then have the law allow you to torture.

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:54.380
We had or we have.

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:59.500
We continue to have something called the the Torture Act of 1946,

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:03.180
which outlaws specifically the torture techniques

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:06.900
that we used against Al Qaeda prisoners in 1946.

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:12.580
We executed Japanese soldiers who had waterboarded American POWs.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:13.820
We executed them.

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:18.220
That was a capital crime to waterboard somebody in 1968.

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:22.780
In January of 1968, the Washington Post ran a front page photograph

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:27.180
of an American soldier waterboarding a North Vietnamese prisoner.

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:31.100
And on the day that that picture ran, the Secretary of Defense,

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:33.620
Robert McNamara ordered an investigation.

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:36.020
The soldier was arrested.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:42.460
He was charged with torture, convicted and sentenced to 20 years at Leavenworth.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:45.820
And now, of course, we live in a nation where Dick Cheney

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:50.900
is openly talking about waterboarding with a character played by Sasha Baron Cohen,

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:52.300
unbeknownst to Cheney.

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:53.660
He thought he was an ally.

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:56.420
Yes, we live in a world where the most powerful people

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:59.820
almost seem to be gleeful when talking about torture.

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:02.860
And at the same time, the the law never changed.

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:05.020
We changed. Right.

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:10.460
And so, you know, why was torture a death penalty offense in 1946?

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:14.540
Worthy of 20 years of hard labor in 1968.

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:17.140
And then in 2002, no, it's fine.

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:18.740
You could just go ahead and do it.

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:21.660
So what what year was it that you joined the CIA?

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:23.380
Was this late 80s, early 90s?

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:26.940
I joined in very late 1989.

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:30.900
My my first day on the job was the day after New Year's 1990.

00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:36.180
And I spent the first seven and a half years as an analyst on Iraq.

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:39.020
Can you explain a little bit about what that means being an analyst?

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:42.060
When it comes to Iraq, obviously, at this point,

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:46.300
Rumsfeld had received the key to the city, basically from Saddam Hussein.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:48.100
Yeah, we hadn't yet gone in.

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:52.580
That's right. HW is Exploration of War within that region.

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:55.940
That, of course, happened there in 91, 92, I believe.

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:57.220
91. Correct.

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:02.740
91. So what were you doing up to that point before our first US

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:05.300
made bombs started exploding over Baghdad?

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:06.580
What was your life like?

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:08.740
That's that's the fun part of the story.

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:13.260
So I was recruited into the CIA by my grad school advisor,

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:16.460
who was undercover as a professor.

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:21.460
Actually, it was actually a CIA officer acting as a spotter,

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:24.540
looking for people who might fit into the CIA's culture.

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:29.020
And he spotted me and recruited me and and I joined the CIA.

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:32.460
That's a fascinating process in itself in hindsight.

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:34.180
Now that you have a little bit of years behind you.

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:34.700
Yeah.

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:38.860
Did you feel as if you were preyed upon or are you do you agree

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:40.700
with their assessment of you?

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:43.140
And do you agree with their reaction with your reaction?

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:46.140
Once they said, look at you, you might just be CIA

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:48.140
because it's kind of like getting into Hogwarts.

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:50.340
It's sort of like your your magic.

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:53.620
It is. And you know, it's not all milk and honey, either.

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:56.740
I learned from a CIA psychiatrist later

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:59.180
that the CIA actively recruits people

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:03.380
who have sociopathic tendencies, not sociopaths.

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:05.060
Right. Of course.

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:07.620
Sociopaths have no conscience.

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:11.500
They blow right through a polygraph, but they're impossible to control

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:16.820
because because they can't stop themselves from from committing criminal acts.

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:19.100
Right. Of course, they get off on deception.

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:22.980
I interviewed somebody who worked with psychopaths and sociopaths,

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:25.420
and he said, you know, that they failed a lie detector test

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:29.220
when after the test is taken, there's a spike because they feel

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:32.460
as if they've just passed, they think they've just gotten away with something.

00:10:32.660 --> 00:10:34.780
So the actual test itself, everyone's like, yeah,

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:36.220
they seem like they're not lying.

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:37.500
But afterwards, you be like, how did I do?

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:38.260
And they'd be like, you passed.

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:39.300
I'm like, yes.

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:40.860
And then they see this massive spike.

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:44.380
And so it's it's a post reaction to them being deceptive.

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:46.500
But in the CIA, you still have to be reliable.

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:48.540
Yeah, you still have to tell the truth.

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:51.620
You know, they told us in that first week on the job,

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:54.100
there are three people you can never lie to.

00:10:54.660 --> 00:11:00.220
You can never lie to medical, finance or security.

00:11:00.860 --> 00:11:02.300
You can lie to your wife.

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:04.620
You shouldn't lie to your boss, but people do.

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:08.020
But medical, finance and security can ruin your life.

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:10.340
And certainly why those three things.

00:11:10.340 --> 00:11:14.020
Because medical, they're all psychiatrists and psychologists.

00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:17.700
And they want to make sure that you're not too nuts to go overseas

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:20.300
and do what they spent a million dollars training you to do.

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:24.540
Finance, listen, intelligence is a cash business.

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:28.700
And I've been, you know, driving down

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:32.700
the road in Pakistan, going through the Khyber Pass

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:36.620
with $10 million in cash in my van and lots and lots of weapons.

00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:39.500
So you can't steal it.

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:41.100
It's not yours.

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:43.340
You've got to account for every cent of it.

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:45.580
And we accounted for every cent.

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:50.140
And then security, you can't pull the dumb and dumber IOU bit

00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:51.940
where you're like, that's for Ferrari.

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:53.260
You're going to want to hold on to that.

00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:54.860
You have to deliver all the money.

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:56.540
And believe me, everybody thinks about it,

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:58.660
but no fooling around with the cash.

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:01.140
And then security is obvious, you know,

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:02.980
they're constantly looking to see,

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:06.300
are you going to go to the Russians or the Chinese or the Israelis

00:12:06.460 --> 00:12:08.780
or the media, which is just as bad?

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:13.620
So so you had seven and a half years working in the analytics,

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:17.100
I guess, area, yes, a field when it comes to Iraq.

00:12:17.260 --> 00:12:19.140
And obviously we can kind of breeze past this

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:22.060
because it seems as if it was torture when it comes to boredom.

00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:25.220
But anything that you picked up on in those seven and a half years

00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:30.260
that we could sort of infer as far as what the future of Iraq

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:34.100
and U.S. relations relations were looking like anything like once

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:37.420
nine 11 happened, were you like, well, it's just a matter of time now?

00:12:37.420 --> 00:12:38.940
Or what were your thoughts?

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:42.140
Um, I wrote about this actually in my first book.

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:44.180
Let me go back.

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:46.700
Let me go back a little bit to to 1990.

00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:49.340
I was given Iraq because nothing ever happened there.

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:53.620
It was the same cabinet since the 1968 revolution.

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:58.460
We would go entire days without a single cable, no press,

00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:02.620
no, no State Department cable, no nothing, nothing would happen there.

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:07.940
And they told me, learn tradecraft, learn the CIA writing style

00:13:07.940 --> 00:13:11.140
and we'll transfer you on to something exciting like Romania.

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:13.260
They actually told me that.

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:17.100
And then just as I got to the point where I knew what I was doing

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:21.140
and I was kind of an expert in the field, Iraq invaded Kuwait

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:23.340
August 2nd, 1990.

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:26.020
So I go into the office seven o'clock that morning.

00:13:26.020 --> 00:13:27.900
I'll never forget it like it was yesterday.

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:31.580
I walk in and my boss says, don't take your jacket off.

00:13:31.580 --> 00:13:33.300
We're going to the White House.

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:36.820
And I kind of thought I was going to end up at the White House that day.

00:13:37.460 --> 00:13:40.740
So we get in a car, we drive to the White House.

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:43.220
You go through these different levels of security

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:45.820
and they take us to the Oval Office.

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:47.620
So I walk in.

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:51.460
It's the president, the vice president, the secretary of state,

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:54.620
the secretary of defense, the national security adviser,

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:57.420
the CIA director, my boss and me.

00:13:58.060 --> 00:13:58.780
Wow.

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:01.380
And so I sit on the couch for a fact.

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:03.820
Most of the people in that room know how to spell potato.

00:14:04.460 --> 00:14:06.460
Not Dan Quayle, not Dan Quayle.

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:08.060
Not Dan Quayle, unfortunately.

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:09.900
That's an insider. It's a very old.

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:11.780
That's a 19 menu. That's a 19.

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:12.900
Yeah, you know, it's funny.

00:14:12.900 --> 00:14:15.180
Dan Quayle was the one guy in that meeting

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:17.220
who never said a single word.

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:18.060
I believe it.

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:19.340
The only is all over his head.

00:14:19.540 --> 00:14:22.540
Yeah, he didn't like what are we talking about now?

00:14:23.020 --> 00:14:25.340
So so you are basically a minor league

00:14:25.340 --> 00:14:27.860
baseball pitcher who just got called up to the Yankees.

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:29.740
And you are in the World Series.

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:31.020
And it's the World Series.

00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:32.620
So I sit on the couch.

00:14:32.620 --> 00:14:36.020
Everybody's sitting around the presidents in his regular chair.

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:40.100
And he says, so now what do we do?

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:42.860
And everybody turns and looks at me.

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:43.900
Oh, my God. Oh, wow.

00:14:43.900 --> 00:14:46.740
Yeah, I'm 25 years old at this point.

00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:47.980
Holy shit.

00:14:47.980 --> 00:14:49.220
So I said, well, Mr.

00:14:49.220 --> 00:14:51.980
President, I said the Kuwaiti government's been overthrown.

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:54.540
Most of them have fled to Saudi Arabia.

00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:59.620
And the Iraqis have installed a puppet government led by.

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:01.500
And I, you know, gave the guy's name.

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:03.340
Well, do we know anything about him?

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:04.220
And I said, yes, sir.

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:05.940
He was the founding member.

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:08.420
He was the co-founding member of the popular front

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:10.660
for the liberation of Palestine.

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:13.900
And Dick Cheney says, Jesus Christ, like that.

00:15:14.540 --> 00:15:16.860
Yeah. And.

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:20.140
You know, the conversation started, we got to push him out.

00:15:20.900 --> 00:15:21.660
I mean, that was it.

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:23.180
We got to push him out.

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:25.620
And what was Cheney's role during the early 90s?

00:15:25.620 --> 00:15:26.260
Secretary of Defense.

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:27.900
Secretary of Defense.

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:29.260
Right. Yeah.

00:15:29.260 --> 00:15:30.620
Secretary of Defense.

00:15:30.620 --> 00:15:34.180
Did you find during that experience, first of all,

00:15:34.780 --> 00:15:38.060
how unnerving was that or how shocking was it

00:15:38.660 --> 00:15:41.700
that you were the one with the answers?

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:43.460
Crazy. 25 years old.

00:15:43.460 --> 00:15:46.660
Crazy. Of course, that would be, you know, remiss, not to mention Dick Cheney,

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:48.860
35 years old, youngest chief of staff of all time.

00:15:48.860 --> 00:15:49.340
That's right.

00:15:49.340 --> 00:15:52.700
And now we have someone 10 years his junior.

00:15:52.940 --> 00:15:55.780
And now you are being asked for the definitive answer

00:15:55.980 --> 00:15:59.740
when it comes to a massive geopolitical fiasco.

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:01.340
Yeah, it was crazy.

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:02.740
And, you know, the first thought.

00:16:02.740 --> 00:16:06.100
And like I say, in my mind, this is like it happened yesterday.

00:16:06.420 --> 00:16:10.100
My first thought was if my buddies from high school could see this,

00:16:10.100 --> 00:16:11.740
they would shit themselves.

00:16:11.740 --> 00:16:15.860
Yeah. Like if I told people, they probably wouldn't even believe me.

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:17.340
Like, what did you do today?

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:20.300
Oh, I believe the entire leadership of the US government.

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:24.740
But I mean, that's that's the situation that you find yourself in.

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:27.780
Just just three years later or two years later,

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:31.780
I'm in the regular morning meeting that we did every morning at nine o'clock.

00:16:31.780 --> 00:16:34.220
Every branch meets to go over what happened overnight.

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:39.260
I'm sitting in there and the secretary walks in and she says, excuse me,

00:16:39.260 --> 00:16:41.580
John, General Powell is on the phone for you.

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:45.820
And I said, General Powell, I said, how does he know who I am?

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:48.620
And she said, I don't know, but he asked for you by name.

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:52.540
And everybody's like, oh, right.

00:16:52.580 --> 00:16:56.940
Well, and let's not forget during this time, this was peak general Powell's power.

00:16:57.020 --> 00:16:59.420
Yeah. I mean, I believe he was on the cover of Time magazine.

00:16:59.420 --> 00:17:02.100
He was around this era being like, hey, you should run for president.

00:17:02.100 --> 00:17:03.860
He was really like this guy.

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:04.900
It really liked him.

00:17:04.900 --> 00:17:07.060
And he was chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff.

00:17:07.420 --> 00:17:11.260
So so I go to my secure phone and I pick it up.

00:17:11.260 --> 00:17:13.260
And I said, good morning, General Powell, may I help you?

00:17:13.940 --> 00:17:17.460
And he said very, very evenly.

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:22.660
He said, John, if the Iraqis were going to kill President Bush,

00:17:22.660 --> 00:17:25.780
this was former President George H.W. Bush, right?

00:17:25.860 --> 00:17:30.700
Who would be the person actually in charge of carrying out that operation?

00:17:31.940 --> 00:17:36.780
And I said, well, if you're talking about the attack that that was foiled in Kuwait,

00:17:37.180 --> 00:17:42.180
I said the Iraqi Intelligence Service Kuwait Station is run from Basra

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:46.260
and Basra Station is personally headed by Abdulaziz Adouri.

00:17:46.660 --> 00:17:48.700
He's the head of the Iraqi Intelligence Service.

00:17:49.300 --> 00:17:51.540
And Powell says, where does he sit?

00:17:51.820 --> 00:17:55.300
And I said, in Iraqi Intelligence Service headquarters in Baghdad,

00:17:55.820 --> 00:17:58.660
he says, thank you. And he hangs up.

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:01.220
OK, I go back in. What did he want?

00:18:01.420 --> 00:18:02.540
I told them what he wanted.

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:06.260
Eight hours later, we fired 48 cruise missiles

00:18:06.260 --> 00:18:08.580
at Iraqi Intelligence Service headquarters.

00:18:09.220 --> 00:18:12.420
Well, how did that make you feel knowing that your words

00:18:13.100 --> 00:18:17.300
manifested themselves in action that, of course, was quite violent?

00:18:18.580 --> 00:18:23.660
You know, at the time I was excited and proud.

00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:28.980
And as time has passed, I've come to the realization

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:33.020
that I was responsible for the death of an innocent janitor that night.

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:37.060
We struck them in the middle of the night, Baghdad time,

00:18:37.820 --> 00:18:40.420
blew the building to smithereens. Right.

00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:43.340
There was one person inside and it was the janitor.

00:18:43.900 --> 00:18:45.900
It's always the fricking janitor.

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:49.060
I believe there was that was the one of the few people that died on 9 11

00:18:49.380 --> 00:18:51.340
during the Pentagon attack was like a janitor.

00:18:51.340 --> 00:18:52.620
And there was a there was a work group.

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:55.540
It's always the people that don't get the memo

00:18:55.780 --> 00:18:58.740
that bombs are about to be flying overhead. Right. Right.

00:18:58.900 --> 00:19:01.100
So I felt very bad about that.

00:19:01.100 --> 00:19:04.740
But I'll tell you, it was Iraq, Iraq, Iraq all the time.

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:08.180
And I got tired because it was clear to me that we weren't serious

00:19:08.220 --> 00:19:10.900
about doing anything about Saddam Hussein,

00:19:10.900 --> 00:19:13.500
at least not through the Clinton administration.

00:19:14.020 --> 00:19:17.740
And so I got bored and I decided to do something else.

00:19:18.620 --> 00:19:22.180
And I wanted to make a transition to operations,

00:19:22.180 --> 00:19:25.220
which was very, very unusual at the time.

00:19:25.220 --> 00:19:30.260
But my hook was I was literally the only person in the CIA

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:32.740
that was fluent in both Arabic and Greek.

00:19:33.620 --> 00:19:37.260
And there was a position that opened in Athens in counterterrorism,

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:41.700
handling, you know, Libyans, Iraqis, Palestinians,

00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:44.220
working these counterterrorism issues. Yeah.

00:19:44.220 --> 00:19:48.820
And so, you know, I went down to the counterterrorism center

00:19:48.820 --> 00:19:51.260
and got the job.

00:19:51.260 --> 00:19:54.380
So what is and of course, let's we can kind of fast forward

00:19:54.380 --> 00:19:56.740
to the early 2000s and one second, but just to clarify,

00:19:56.740 --> 00:20:00.220
because this is a whole other world that I'm not privy to.

00:20:00.740 --> 00:20:02.460
You went from analytics to operations.

00:20:02.460 --> 00:20:06.220
What is the exact difference as far as jobs and how what does that entail?

00:20:06.540 --> 00:20:08.780
Right. Well, that's actually a very good question.

00:20:09.580 --> 00:20:13.060
And when I when I told my boss on the analytics side

00:20:13.060 --> 00:20:15.660
that I was going to switch to operations,

00:20:15.660 --> 00:20:17.620
the first thing he said to me was,

00:20:17.620 --> 00:20:19.980
are you sure you know what you're getting into?

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:22.500
And I said, yeah, man, I'm so bored.

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:25.540
I said, I just sit here and drink coffee all day long.

00:20:26.060 --> 00:20:29.100
And he said, you know, they're going to train you to kill people.

00:20:29.100 --> 00:20:33.740
Right. And I said, Ben, you watch too many movies, buddy.

00:20:34.740 --> 00:20:36.500
And I laughed.

00:20:36.500 --> 00:20:39.980
Two weeks later, I go to training at the CIA's facility,

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:42.220
colloquially known as the farm, right?

00:20:43.660 --> 00:20:46.580
And they're training me to kill people.

00:20:46.580 --> 00:20:51.020
And I'm standing there thinking you have got to be kidding me.

00:20:51.740 --> 00:20:55.260
And there was one point where, you know, they they taught us.

00:20:57.300 --> 00:21:00.980
You know, how to jump out of planes to parachute into enemy territory

00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:02.380
and what to do if you're captured.

00:21:02.380 --> 00:21:05.020
And, you know, how to withstand torture.

00:21:05.620 --> 00:21:08.260
Well, not really withstand it, but as long as you can.

00:21:09.100 --> 00:21:12.860
And I remember saying to the instructor, this is pre 9-11, of course.

00:21:12.900 --> 00:21:17.420
So I said, listen, I'm going to be recruiting people at diplomatic cocktail parties.

00:21:18.140 --> 00:21:21.180
I'm not going to be jumping out of planes behind enemy lines.

00:21:22.740 --> 00:21:25.340
Two years later, 9-11's happened.

00:21:25.900 --> 00:21:29.220
I'm in a plane and I turned to this guy next to me and I said,

00:21:29.780 --> 00:21:32.180
I can't freaking believe I'm going to jump out of this plane.

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:35.140
Wow. Wow. That's insane.

00:21:35.460 --> 00:21:38.420
You mentioned torture and obviously the CIA.

00:21:39.180 --> 00:21:42.060
I'm not sure how you train someone to withstand torture.

00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:43.540
I don't know what that process is.

00:21:43.540 --> 00:21:44.940
It can't be very comfortable.

00:21:44.940 --> 00:21:46.380
No, it's awful.

00:21:46.380 --> 00:21:47.980
But let's let's talk about that.

00:21:47.980 --> 00:21:49.980
So 9-11 happens.

00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:51.900
You are you're again, you're working in operations.

00:21:51.900 --> 00:21:53.220
Are you still in Athens at this time?

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:54.140
You're back in the States.

00:21:54.140 --> 00:21:59.180
No, I returned to headquarters from Athens in the summer of 2000.

00:21:59.740 --> 00:22:00.860
OK.

00:22:00.860 --> 00:22:05.140
And and I was working in a group

00:22:05.140 --> 00:22:09.900
that trained Middle Eastern intelligence services in counterterrorism operations.

00:22:10.260 --> 00:22:12.700
OK, so kind of back to a desk job in some ways,

00:22:12.700 --> 00:22:14.900
sipping a little bit more coffee than you'd like, most likely.

00:22:14.900 --> 00:22:17.180
Yeah, lots of travel to cool places.

00:22:17.180 --> 00:22:19.020
So it was worth it. OK, great.

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:22.420
So September 11th, you know, let's say 7.55 in the morning,

00:22:22.420 --> 00:22:27.260
Eastern Standard Time, planes begin to, you know, do what they did to the towers.

00:22:27.780 --> 00:22:28.380
Go on, please.

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:31.140
I had I had a meeting that day with Condoleezza Rice.

00:22:31.140 --> 00:22:34.100
I was going down to the White House with Kofir Black,

00:22:34.300 --> 00:22:37.980
Ambassador Kofir Black, who was the director of the CIA's Counterterrorism Center.

00:22:38.460 --> 00:22:40.660
And, you know, it's funny.

00:22:42.860 --> 00:22:46.180
The the the White House ordered the publication

00:22:46.180 --> 00:22:49.260
and the declassification of these historical

00:22:50.380 --> 00:22:53.740
cables from 1947 to 1969.

00:22:54.260 --> 00:22:58.660
And we found three of the three of these cables outed sources of ours

00:22:58.660 --> 00:23:00.100
who were still alive.

00:23:00.100 --> 00:23:03.660
And so we were going to talk to Condoleezza Rice about

00:23:04.780 --> 00:23:08.140
pulling these cables out of the finished product.

00:23:09.580 --> 00:23:14.740
So she kept saying, no, no, no, she was the National Security Advisor at the time.

00:23:15.060 --> 00:23:16.660
No, we're not going to do that.

00:23:16.660 --> 00:23:19.100
Transparency, declassification.

00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:23.340
So Kofir said, let's let's just go down there and make the pitch.

00:23:23.340 --> 00:23:27.500
I said, great. So I got a call saying our car was ready to take us to the White House.

00:23:27.500 --> 00:23:30.660
So I walked over to Kofir's office to tell him it was time to go.

00:23:30.900 --> 00:23:34.460
And his secretary had a TV on her desk when that was a novel thing.

00:23:34.900 --> 00:23:38.980
And and one of the towers of the World Trade Center was burning.

00:23:38.980 --> 00:23:41.060
And I said, what happened in World Trade Center?

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:43.540
And she said, oh, a plane flew into it.

00:23:44.100 --> 00:23:46.180
And I said, because I'm an idiot.

00:23:46.660 --> 00:23:50.500
I said, you know, that happened once before in 1930,

00:23:50.500 --> 00:23:53.220
a plane flew into the Empire State Building,

00:23:53.540 --> 00:23:55.980
but it was really rainy and foggy that day.

00:23:55.980 --> 00:23:57.420
And it's so clear today.

00:23:57.420 --> 00:24:00.460
How can you not see that you're flying into the World Trade Center?

00:24:01.460 --> 00:24:04.220
And just as I said that, the second plane hit.

00:24:04.940 --> 00:24:09.580
Wow. And she turned to me and she said, did you see that?

00:24:09.580 --> 00:24:11.620
Or did I imagine it?

00:24:11.620 --> 00:24:14.980
Wow. And I ran back to my office and I said, guys,

00:24:15.340 --> 00:24:17.940
two planes just hit both towers of the World Trade Center.

00:24:17.940 --> 00:24:19.900
We're under attack.

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:22.740
And all hell broke loose from there.

00:24:23.260 --> 00:24:25.140
Can you explain a little bit about what that?

00:24:25.140 --> 00:24:26.900
What do you mean when all hell breaks loose?

00:24:26.900 --> 00:24:30.100
Well, everybody in the Counterterrorism Center ran to the front office

00:24:30.100 --> 00:24:32.620
and we had TVs mounted along the ceiling.

00:24:32.620 --> 00:24:36.140
They were all on different networks, US and and foreign networks.

00:24:36.140 --> 00:24:38.340
And, you know, it was just.

00:24:39.300 --> 00:24:40.620
It was just developing.

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:44.660
There were reports that a car bomb had gone off at the State Department

00:24:44.900 --> 00:24:47.620
that somebody had fired a rocket at the White House.

00:24:48.100 --> 00:24:52.940
And it was just if everybody was standing there in silence

00:24:52.940 --> 00:24:57.100
and somebody behind me shouted, will somebody please lead?

00:24:57.900 --> 00:25:00.180
And it was like somebody had slapped Kofur.

00:25:00.380 --> 00:25:06.300
So he says, you go to the director's office and say this, you go to the security

00:25:06.300 --> 00:25:08.340
and tell them that you go to, you know.

00:25:09.540 --> 00:25:12.300
And so finally, a couple of cops came, you know,

00:25:12.300 --> 00:25:17.100
CIA has its own police force called the Special Protective Officers.

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:22.060
And so these two Spos came to the to the office and said,

00:25:22.060 --> 00:25:26.540
everybody out, everybody evacuate, nobody, nobody budged.

00:25:27.860 --> 00:25:31.740
Kofur got up on his desk and he said, we all know who did this.

00:25:32.380 --> 00:25:35.980
He said, we're at war today and we're all going to have to do our part.

00:25:36.420 --> 00:25:38.940
Not all of us are going to make it home.

00:25:38.940 --> 00:25:43.580
So if this isn't for you, no one will think less of you if you walk out now

00:25:44.220 --> 00:25:45.900
and nobody budged.

00:25:45.900 --> 00:25:49.740
Finally, the cops came back and said, if we didn't evacuate, we would be arrested.

00:25:50.820 --> 00:25:52.820
So we evacuated.

00:25:52.820 --> 00:25:57.380
So when it comes to Mr. Black, what was, who did he think?

00:25:58.020 --> 00:26:00.420
Did it? I mean, was he thinking Iraq?

00:26:00.420 --> 00:26:01.900
Was he thinking? No, no, no, no, no, no.

00:26:01.900 --> 00:26:05.100
Nobody, nobody believed Iraq had anything to do with this,

00:26:05.100 --> 00:26:08.500
except Dick Janney and the minions around him.

00:26:08.980 --> 00:26:11.380
And any verbiage about the Saudis?

00:26:11.380 --> 00:26:14.780
Well, a lot of verbiage about the Saudis.

00:26:14.980 --> 00:26:20.660
Listen, on July 6th, right, two months before 9-11, July 6th, 2001,

00:26:21.140 --> 00:26:24.780
I was hosting a group of Middle Eastern intelligence officers.

00:26:24.780 --> 00:26:29.780
And this was a normal daily liaison exchange, right?

00:26:29.780 --> 00:26:33.020
They fly in, we give them a day of briefings.

00:26:33.220 --> 00:26:35.540
They get a photo op with the director.

00:26:35.540 --> 00:26:39.580
We exchange gifts and then we take them out to Ruth's Chris Steakhouse.

00:26:39.660 --> 00:26:42.020
We do this every single day, right?

00:26:42.020 --> 00:26:43.820
Much more of just a diplomatic affair.

00:26:43.860 --> 00:26:44.820
Absolutely.

00:26:44.820 --> 00:26:46.100
Absolutely.

00:26:46.100 --> 00:26:49.740
So we had this group come in on July 6th,

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:56.180
and I asked the junior analyst on Al Qaeda to come in and give a briefing.

00:26:57.340 --> 00:27:01.540
So it came time for this briefing and in Wax Cofer

00:27:02.140 --> 00:27:05.740
and the counterterrorism center's chief of operations.

00:27:06.380 --> 00:27:10.900
And I was shocked and I stood up and I said, oh, I said, gentlemen,

00:27:10.900 --> 00:27:12.300
this is this is Mr.

00:27:12.300 --> 00:27:15.820
Cofer Black, he's the director of the CIA's counterterrorism center.

00:27:16.460 --> 00:27:19.380
And I introduced these guys to Cofer.

00:27:19.700 --> 00:27:21.340
I was shocked.

00:27:21.340 --> 00:27:24.700
Yeah. And he sat down and he said, and I'll never forget it.

00:27:25.180 --> 00:27:27.940
It was he was so impassioned.

00:27:28.020 --> 00:27:31.740
He said, gentlemen, something terrible is going to happen.

00:27:32.340 --> 00:27:36.740
We don't know when and we don't know where, but we know it's going to be massive.

00:27:37.340 --> 00:27:40.860
He said, the mood in the camps is one of jubilation.

00:27:41.540 --> 00:27:45.700
We are hearing camp commanders on the phone with their students

00:27:45.700 --> 00:27:49.260
and they're crying and saying, I'll see you in paradise.

00:27:49.740 --> 00:27:52.700
They're using code words for a massive attack.

00:27:53.340 --> 00:27:56.900
The honey salesman is coming with vast quantities of honey.

00:27:57.260 --> 00:27:59.140
There's going to be a great wedding.

00:27:59.140 --> 00:28:01.460
There's going to be a great football game.

00:28:01.740 --> 00:28:04.660
He said, these are all codes for a huge attack.

00:28:05.300 --> 00:28:08.100
And he said, we just don't know when or where.

00:28:08.460 --> 00:28:15.500
And he said, I beg you, if you have any sources inside Al Qaeda, please help us.

00:28:16.540 --> 00:28:18.860
And they just sat there.

00:28:18.860 --> 00:28:21.740
So what did that mean to you when they just sat there?

00:28:22.220 --> 00:28:23.980
That they didn't have any sources.

00:28:23.980 --> 00:28:26.620
Just like we didn't think that they were just like they were deceptive.

00:28:26.620 --> 00:28:29.140
OK. Yeah. No.

00:28:29.140 --> 00:28:32.380
Al Qaeda's operational security was so excellent

00:28:33.180 --> 00:28:35.340
that no one could infiltrate them.

00:28:36.020 --> 00:28:37.460
We just couldn't do it.

00:28:37.460 --> 00:28:42.460
So at the end of the day, I sent these guys back to the hotel before dinner

00:28:42.460 --> 00:28:44.700
and I went to Cofer's office to thank him.

00:28:44.700 --> 00:28:47.660
And I said, Cofer, I got to ask you, I don't work on Al Qaeda.

00:28:48.340 --> 00:28:50.940
Was that just for their benefit or were you serious?

00:28:51.460 --> 00:28:53.260
And he said, oh, I was serious.

00:28:53.260 --> 00:28:55.780
Something terrible is going to happen.

00:28:55.780 --> 00:28:57.460
And then September 11th, there it is.

00:28:57.460 --> 00:28:59.460
It's just unfolding in front of us.

00:28:59.460 --> 00:29:01.180
Oh, my God. So all of these.

00:29:01.180 --> 00:29:05.020
So do you sort of go back in your mind also when you think of Al Qaeda

00:29:05.060 --> 00:29:07.860
that we on last podcast on the left, the other show that I do,

00:29:07.860 --> 00:29:09.100
we talk a lot about cults.

00:29:09.100 --> 00:29:12.020
And that seems as if they were in the waning moments of a death cult.

00:29:12.700 --> 00:29:14.620
Where is jubilation? It is joy.

00:29:14.620 --> 00:29:19.220
That's a good way to say it because Osama bin Laden had literally no religious

00:29:19.220 --> 00:29:22.860
credentials, none. He wasn't any mom.

00:29:23.100 --> 00:29:26.300
He wasn't learned in the Koran, none.

00:29:27.100 --> 00:29:29.820
He was a cult leader. That's really what it came down to.

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:34.620
The all new tropical butterfly refresher is now at Starbucks.

00:29:35.020 --> 00:29:39.260
Dive into juicy guava and pasture fruit flavors with mango pineapple

00:29:39.260 --> 00:29:43.940
popping pearls bursting in every sip, ice cold, instantly refreshing

00:29:43.940 --> 00:29:48.100
and impossible to put down made for summer only at Starbucks.

00:29:49.780 --> 00:29:51.180
Hey, it's Ryan Reynolds here from it mobile.

00:29:51.180 --> 00:29:55.500
Now I was looking for fun ways to tell you that Mint's offer of unlimited

00:29:55.500 --> 00:29:58.020
premium wireless for $15 a month is back.

00:29:58.380 --> 00:30:03.420
So I thought it would be fun if we made $15 bills, but it turns out

00:30:03.980 --> 00:30:05.900
that's very illegal.

00:30:05.900 --> 00:30:07.860
So there goes my big idea for the commercial.

00:30:07.860 --> 00:30:12.300
Give it a try at mintmobile.com slash switch up from payment of $45 for three

