equivalent taxes and fees extra initial plan term only greater than

00:30:17.820 --> 00:30:19.220
50 gigabytes may slow when network is busy.

00:30:19.220 --> 00:30:20.100
Sea terms.

00:30:20.100 --> 00:30:23.140
So as 9 11 is occurring and unfolded in front of your eyes,

00:30:23.140 --> 00:30:26.020
as you mentioned, you have these flashbacks to these moments

00:30:26.020 --> 00:30:29.260
that you maybe thought were innocuous and that really mattered.

00:30:29.300 --> 00:30:32.460
Oh, yeah, let's go let's go forward a little bit then as

00:30:33.300 --> 00:30:35.980
some of the policy decisions that were being made at that time

00:30:35.980 --> 00:30:41.260
by mostly Dick Cheney, you know, and NW was in my opinion, more of a mouthpiece.

00:30:41.260 --> 00:30:42.660
And I don't think he's stupid by any means.

00:30:42.660 --> 00:30:45.780
I actually think that's a gross understatement because he is intelligent

00:30:46.260 --> 00:30:47.100
in his own right.

00:30:47.100 --> 00:30:48.900
And God knows agree with all that.

00:30:48.900 --> 00:30:51.580
Yeah, he has caused the policies that he created.

00:30:51.580 --> 00:30:52.260
But anyway, go on.

00:30:52.260 --> 00:30:54.620
But but listen, but it was clear.

00:30:54.620 --> 00:30:57.940
It was clear to us inside the CIA that Dick Cheney was the president.

00:30:58.300 --> 00:31:00.140
Yeah, right. It was clear.

00:31:00.140 --> 00:31:04.700
So so with that knowledge and sort of knowing the hawkish nature of Dick,

00:31:05.340 --> 00:31:08.700
as things began to transpire, we know what 9 11 was.

00:31:08.700 --> 00:31:10.660
I mean, don't forget. I mean, I'm sure no one else.

00:31:10.660 --> 00:31:11.620
No one here will forget.

00:31:11.620 --> 00:31:14.420
But, you know, the buildings were smoldering for weeks.

00:31:14.460 --> 00:31:17.740
Yeah. And the US and the American people and the world were like,

00:31:17.740 --> 00:31:19.020
what the hell is going on?

00:31:19.020 --> 00:31:23.660
We had massive support as the plans were being drawn up

00:31:23.660 --> 00:31:26.060
as far as a reaction to the 9 11 attacks.

00:31:26.540 --> 00:31:27.500
What were your thoughts?

00:31:27.500 --> 00:31:30.460
Were did you think the US was going in the right direction?

00:31:30.460 --> 00:31:34.940
Obviously, I think 99 percent of what we'll say 95.

00:31:35.220 --> 00:31:38.740
We'll say 90 percent of people agreed with Afghanistan.

00:31:38.860 --> 00:31:41.820
Yeah, the Afghanistan war, I think, did have quite a bit of

00:31:42.700 --> 00:31:45.020
it was by parts. Yeah, it was.

00:31:45.020 --> 00:31:47.580
But as things were transpiring, what were your thoughts?

00:31:47.580 --> 00:31:49.580
As all of a sudden, we hear Iraq, all of a sudden, we hear

00:31:49.580 --> 00:31:52.500
Axis of Evil, all of these sorts of things were in terror.

00:31:52.500 --> 00:31:57.100
Well, I volunteered to go to Iraq repeatedly.

00:31:57.500 --> 00:32:00.620
I kept telling myself, my Arabic is excellent.

00:32:01.020 --> 00:32:03.260
Who's going to be interrogating these prisoners?

00:32:03.260 --> 00:32:06.780
Right. As we as we start capturing these al-Qaeda fighters

00:32:06.780 --> 00:32:08.940
and al-Qaeda leaders, who's interrogating them?

00:32:09.500 --> 00:32:15.020
And about six weeks after 9 11, I run into an old friend

00:32:15.020 --> 00:32:16.300
of mine in the hall.

00:32:16.300 --> 00:32:19.660
He's a 70 was a 72 year old contractor.

00:32:19.660 --> 00:32:20.700
He's dead now.

00:32:20.700 --> 00:32:23.820
But I said, hey, man, where you been?

00:32:23.820 --> 00:32:26.940
And he looks around, he says, I've been in Afghanistan.

00:32:27.340 --> 00:32:29.580
And I said, really, what do you do in there?

00:32:30.460 --> 00:32:31.740
And he looks at me like I'm crazy.

00:32:31.740 --> 00:32:33.900
And he says, I've been killing people.

00:32:33.900 --> 00:32:35.500
What do you think I've been doing?

00:32:36.860 --> 00:32:40.540
And I said, oh, my God, that's why they haven't sent me.

00:32:41.260 --> 00:32:43.180
They don't need any translator.

00:32:43.660 --> 00:32:45.660
They're not capturing anybody.

00:32:46.220 --> 00:32:50.380
They're just killing everyone that they come into contact with.

00:32:50.620 --> 00:32:52.540
That they come into contact with.

00:32:52.540 --> 00:32:55.660
So I went into the into Cofer's deputy's office.

00:32:55.660 --> 00:32:57.500
I had worked for him a couple of years earlier.

00:32:58.140 --> 00:33:00.140
And I made this totally idle threat.

00:33:01.100 --> 00:33:05.740
I said, if you don't send me to Afghanistan right now,

00:33:05.740 --> 00:33:09.420
I am walking straight to Exxon with my Arabic

00:33:09.420 --> 00:33:10.940
and I am not looking back.

00:33:12.460 --> 00:33:14.540
And he's like, take it easy.

00:33:15.260 --> 00:33:17.980
He goes, can you go to Pakistan?

00:33:17.980 --> 00:33:19.340
And I said, yes, when?

00:33:19.980 --> 00:33:21.100
He said, tomorrow.

00:33:21.100 --> 00:33:21.900
I said, yes.

00:33:21.900 --> 00:33:22.780
What do you want me to do?

00:33:23.340 --> 00:33:25.340
He said, I want you to be chief of counterterrorism

00:33:25.340 --> 00:33:26.380
operations there.

00:33:26.380 --> 00:33:27.180
I said, done.

00:33:28.220 --> 00:33:28.940
Can I leave now?

00:33:28.940 --> 00:33:29.740
I'm going to go pack.

00:33:30.380 --> 00:33:31.180
And he goes, go.

00:33:31.820 --> 00:33:33.180
So I called my girlfriend.

00:33:33.180 --> 00:33:35.100
She later became my wife and I said,

00:33:35.100 --> 00:33:36.780
I got to go to Pakistan tomorrow.

00:33:36.780 --> 00:33:38.620
Can you meet me at my place and help me pack?

00:33:39.260 --> 00:33:40.700
So she met me there.

00:33:40.700 --> 00:33:42.540
I packed next day.

00:33:42.540 --> 00:33:46.700
I flew to Pakistan and I took over

00:33:46.700 --> 00:33:48.220
counterterrorism operations there.

00:33:48.940 --> 00:33:52.300
So I was there seven months.

00:33:52.300 --> 00:33:54.700
I'm going to make a very long story, very short.

00:33:55.420 --> 00:33:58.220
And we captured Abu Zubaydah, who we believed

00:33:58.220 --> 00:34:00.140
was the number three in al-Qaeda.

00:34:00.140 --> 00:34:03.340
He was not the number three, but he was a very bad man.

00:34:03.340 --> 00:34:08.060
He had founded and run al-Qaeda's two training camps

00:34:08.060 --> 00:34:09.260
in southern Afghanistan.

00:34:09.900 --> 00:34:13.660
And he had founded and run the House of Martyrs

00:34:14.860 --> 00:34:18.060
safe house, al-Qaeda safe house in Peshawar, Pakistan.

00:34:18.300 --> 00:34:19.980
He was the logistics guy.

00:34:19.980 --> 00:34:21.900
If you wanted to get into Afghanistan

00:34:21.900 --> 00:34:24.220
to fight the Americans, he got you in.

00:34:24.220 --> 00:34:27.100
If you were tired of the fight, he got you out.

00:34:27.100 --> 00:34:29.020
You wanted to go home.

00:34:29.020 --> 00:34:32.860
He got you a fake passport and a plane ticket

00:34:32.860 --> 00:34:34.300
to wherever you came from.

00:34:34.300 --> 00:34:35.980
So he was a very bad guy.

00:34:36.540 --> 00:34:37.740
We caught him.

00:34:37.740 --> 00:34:39.420
We sent him to a secret prison.

00:34:40.620 --> 00:34:42.860
And I returned to headquarters.

00:34:42.860 --> 00:34:45.100
Now this is where I can answer your question.

00:34:46.140 --> 00:34:47.820
I got the headquarters at the end of May.

00:34:48.380 --> 00:34:49.420
2002.

00:34:50.300 --> 00:34:54.060
And I was in the cafeteria one day, getting lunch.

00:34:55.500 --> 00:34:57.980
And one of the big shots from the counter-terrorism center

00:34:57.980 --> 00:35:00.060
came up to me very casually.

00:35:00.620 --> 00:35:02.940
And he said, hey, I'm glad I ran into you.

00:35:02.940 --> 00:35:05.500
I wanted to ask, would you be interested

00:35:05.500 --> 00:35:08.220
in being trained in enhanced interrogation techniques?

00:35:08.940 --> 00:35:10.620
And I had never heard that term before.

00:35:11.260 --> 00:35:13.500
And I said, enhanced interrogation techniques.

00:35:13.500 --> 00:35:14.300
What does that mean?

00:35:15.020 --> 00:35:16.780
And he says, we're going to start getting

00:35:16.780 --> 00:35:17.820
rough with these guys.

00:35:19.020 --> 00:35:20.460
And I said, well, what does that mean?

00:35:21.100 --> 00:35:23.660
And he laid out these 10 techniques.

00:35:24.540 --> 00:35:26.300
I said, oh, I don't know, man, that sounds

00:35:26.300 --> 00:35:27.420
like a torture program.

00:35:27.420 --> 00:35:29.340
Can you go through some of those techniques

00:35:29.340 --> 00:35:31.260
that kind of specifically stood out

00:35:31.260 --> 00:35:33.180
where you're like, oh, red flag, red flag.

00:35:33.180 --> 00:35:35.100
Well, waterboarding was the famous one,

00:35:35.100 --> 00:35:36.460
or the one that became famous.

00:35:36.940 --> 00:35:38.460
But I thought that there were two

00:35:38.460 --> 00:35:39.980
that were worse than waterboarding.

00:35:41.420 --> 00:35:44.860
One was sleep deprivation.

00:35:45.500 --> 00:35:49.100
We know from the American Psychological Association

00:35:49.100 --> 00:35:52.060
that people begin to lose their minds

00:35:52.060 --> 00:35:54.700
at day seven with no sleep.

00:35:55.740 --> 00:35:58.060
They begin to die at day nine.

00:35:58.060 --> 00:36:02.220
Your organs, you go into organ failure at day nine.

00:36:02.220 --> 00:36:04.140
But the CIA was authorized to keep

00:36:04.140 --> 00:36:05.900
people awake for 12 days.

00:36:06.700 --> 00:36:09.500
Now, this is with you changed to an eyeball

00:36:09.500 --> 00:36:10.620
in the ceiling.

00:36:10.620 --> 00:36:13.100
So you can't kneel or sit or lay

00:36:13.100 --> 00:36:14.380
or get comfortable in any way.

00:36:15.500 --> 00:36:19.500
Industrial strength lights beaming down on you

00:36:19.500 --> 00:36:23.580
and then just death metal blasting at you 24 hours a day.

00:36:23.580 --> 00:36:24.300
You go crazy.

00:36:26.300 --> 00:36:28.060
The other one was called the cold cell

00:36:28.060 --> 00:36:30.060
where you're chained to that eyeballed

00:36:30.060 --> 00:36:31.180
in the ceiling again.

00:36:31.180 --> 00:36:32.220
You're stripped naked.

00:36:32.780 --> 00:36:35.740
Your cell is chilled to 50 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:36:35.740 --> 00:36:37.900
And every hour somebody goes into the cell

00:36:37.900 --> 00:36:40.140
and throws a bucket of ice water on you.

00:36:40.140 --> 00:36:42.700
We killed two people with that technique.

00:36:42.700 --> 00:36:44.780
It reminds me of unit 731,

00:36:45.980 --> 00:36:48.620
which was a horrible unit in Japan

00:36:48.620 --> 00:36:51.100
during World War II, I believe,

00:36:51.100 --> 00:36:52.620
where they would do similar things,

00:36:52.620 --> 00:36:54.300
leave people out in the cold, dump water on their hands,

00:36:54.300 --> 00:36:55.900
then they would shatter the ice off of their hands,

00:36:55.900 --> 00:36:58.380
obviously breaking their fingers and everything like that.

00:36:59.020 --> 00:37:01.500
So when you hear the enhanced interrogation,

00:37:01.500 --> 00:37:04.140
again, a cute word for torture, you're in there,

00:37:04.140 --> 00:37:05.500
you're just surrounded by,

00:37:05.500 --> 00:37:07.420
I assume, some kind of jello mold.

00:37:07.420 --> 00:37:09.820
I don't know what people eat in the White House.

00:37:10.780 --> 00:37:13.820
Did things change for you that day?

00:37:13.820 --> 00:37:16.780
Yes, they changed for me quite literally that day

00:37:16.780 --> 00:37:19.100
because I turned down the training.

00:37:19.100 --> 00:37:21.100
And of the 14 people that they asked,

00:37:21.100 --> 00:37:23.260
I was the only one who turned it down.

00:37:23.260 --> 00:37:24.620
And you're the same guy who was like,

00:37:24.620 --> 00:37:26.380
send me to fucking Afghanistan.

00:37:26.380 --> 00:37:27.580
Okay, I'll take Pakistan.

00:37:27.580 --> 00:37:28.780
And it wasn't like you were,

00:37:28.780 --> 00:37:31.180
you know, resting on your laurels or sitting on your ass.

00:37:31.180 --> 00:37:33.180
I had just caught the number three al Qaeda.

00:37:33.900 --> 00:37:35.740
And so because I turned down the training,

00:37:35.740 --> 00:37:37.260
they passed me over for promotion.

00:37:38.300 --> 00:37:39.340
Oh my gosh.

00:37:39.340 --> 00:37:39.820
Yeah.

00:37:39.820 --> 00:37:41.660
And I complained and I said,

00:37:41.660 --> 00:37:42.540
what do I have to do?

00:37:42.540 --> 00:37:46.140
I have to catch bin Laden to get promoted around here.

00:37:46.140 --> 00:37:47.100
But what happened was,

00:37:47.820 --> 00:37:53.020
I had been such a star from Pakistan that they sent me up

00:37:53.020 --> 00:37:55.100
to the seventh floor, the executive level,

00:37:55.100 --> 00:37:57.100
and I became the executive assistant

00:37:57.100 --> 00:37:58.780
to the CIA's deputy director.

00:37:59.820 --> 00:38:03.420
So he gave me an out of cycle promotion.

00:38:04.220 --> 00:38:07.580
And in that job, I got to see literally everything

00:38:08.140 --> 00:38:10.620
that the CIA was doing everywhere in the world.

00:38:10.620 --> 00:38:12.460
Now my very first day in that job,

00:38:13.340 --> 00:38:15.980
I walked in to the deputy director's office

00:38:16.540 --> 00:38:19.900
and I said, okay, so what do we do?

00:38:21.260 --> 00:38:25.260
And he said, can't tell you until you sign

00:38:25.260 --> 00:38:27.580
your secrecy agreements, you need to go down to room,

00:38:27.580 --> 00:38:29.900
you know, 606 or whatever it was,

00:38:29.900 --> 00:38:32.540
they have your secrecy agreements there for you to sign.

00:38:32.540 --> 00:38:33.500
So I go down there.

00:38:35.020 --> 00:38:36.780
They're all laid out on a table.

00:38:36.860 --> 00:38:38.860
And I knew the guy, the security officer.

00:38:38.860 --> 00:38:41.420
So I sign all six of these agreements saying

00:38:41.420 --> 00:38:44.780
that I'll never breathe a word of this as long as I live,

00:38:44.780 --> 00:38:46.300
unless it's declassified.

00:38:47.260 --> 00:38:50.460
So I sign all six and I said, okay, so what's up?

00:38:51.740 --> 00:38:52.940
And he takes this deep breath.

00:38:52.940 --> 00:39:01.580
He goes, well, next year in March, we're going to invade Iraq.

00:39:02.220 --> 00:39:04.060
We're going to overthrow Saddam Hussein.

00:39:04.860 --> 00:39:07.900
We're going to open the world's largest air force base.

00:39:08.540 --> 00:39:12.540
And we're going to move all of our air assets out of Saudi Arabia

00:39:12.540 --> 00:39:17.100
into Iraq so that we can deprive Osama bin Laden

00:39:17.100 --> 00:39:20.460
of the ability of saying that we're polluting

00:39:20.460 --> 00:39:22.140
the land of the two holy mosques.

00:39:23.980 --> 00:39:26.300
And I looked at him, I was like speechless.

00:39:26.940 --> 00:39:31.180
And all I could, all I could blurt out was we haven't caught

00:39:31.180 --> 00:39:31.980
bin Laden yet.

00:39:33.340 --> 00:39:36.220
And he said, John, the battle lines have been drawn.

00:39:36.220 --> 00:39:37.820
The decisions have been made.

00:39:37.820 --> 00:39:39.980
And he said, and there are two sides to this.

00:39:40.620 --> 00:39:43.740
He said the side that doesn't want to attack Iraq

00:39:43.740 --> 00:39:47.740
is the CIA, the State Department, and the Joint Chiefs of Staff.

00:39:48.620 --> 00:39:51.900
And the warmongers are the Office of the Vice President,

00:39:51.900 --> 00:39:53.900
the Office of the Secretary of Defense,

00:39:53.900 --> 00:39:55.820
and the National Security Council.

00:39:55.820 --> 00:39:57.420
And he said, and guess what?

00:39:57.420 --> 00:39:58.060
We lose.

00:39:58.860 --> 00:40:00.780
Oh, man.

00:40:00.860 --> 00:40:01.180
Yeah.

00:40:01.180 --> 00:40:04.860
So in some ways, you have more power than obviously

00:40:04.860 --> 00:40:06.380
the vast majority of civilians.

00:40:06.380 --> 00:40:06.700
Oh, yeah.

00:40:06.700 --> 00:40:09.500
But then in others, you're just as knee-capped

00:40:09.500 --> 00:40:11.660
and handcuffed by the policies as all of us.

00:40:11.660 --> 00:40:13.980
The CIA is a support agency.

00:40:14.700 --> 00:40:17.420
They do what the White House tells them to do.

00:40:18.940 --> 00:40:19.900
It's as simple as that.

00:40:19.900 --> 00:40:22.460
I went back to the deputy director's office

00:40:23.260 --> 00:40:26.620
and I walked in and he said, they read you in, huh?

00:40:26.620 --> 00:40:28.380
Meaning I signed the documents.

00:40:28.380 --> 00:40:31.020
And I said, are they out of their minds?

00:40:31.900 --> 00:40:33.740
And he said, yes, they are.

00:40:33.740 --> 00:40:34.780
And I'll tell you another thing.

00:40:36.060 --> 00:40:37.820
George Tennant was the director at the time.

00:40:38.700 --> 00:40:41.340
And because Iraq became my issue,

00:40:41.340 --> 00:40:44.060
I was the executive assistant for Iraq.

00:40:45.900 --> 00:40:48.300
I was the note-taker in all of George's meetings

00:40:48.300 --> 00:40:49.420
with the principals.

00:40:50.300 --> 00:40:54.860
So here we are, the day before we invade Iraq.

00:40:54.860 --> 00:40:56.460
It may have been two days before.

00:40:57.340 --> 00:40:59.500
And we're doing a secure video teleconference.

00:40:59.500 --> 00:41:01.980
So George is sitting at the head of the table.

00:41:01.980 --> 00:41:04.860
I'm sitting directly behind him, taking notes.

00:41:04.860 --> 00:41:06.860
So on all the video screens,

00:41:06.860 --> 00:41:11.260
here's Cheney and Condi Rice and General,

00:41:11.260 --> 00:41:13.100
whoever it was at the time, General Hor,

00:41:13.100 --> 00:41:17.340
maybe I don't remember, and Colin Powell

00:41:17.340 --> 00:41:19.180
and the head of NSA.

00:41:19.180 --> 00:41:20.780
And it's all the principals, right?

00:41:22.300 --> 00:41:24.700
So George is just sitting there like this.

00:41:25.500 --> 00:41:27.820
And because we're a support agency,

00:41:27.820 --> 00:41:31.100
the CIA director's job is to answer a question

00:41:31.100 --> 00:41:33.020
if one is asked.

00:41:33.020 --> 00:41:35.340
Otherwise, you sit there silently

00:41:35.340 --> 00:41:37.580
because we're not policymakers.

00:41:37.580 --> 00:41:39.580
The policy comes from the White House

00:41:39.580 --> 00:41:41.420
and the departments of state and defense.

00:41:42.300 --> 00:41:48.300
So they asked General, whoever it was, General,

00:41:48.300 --> 00:41:49.420
I'm going to have to look it up

00:41:49.420 --> 00:41:50.300
because I can't remember.

00:41:50.300 --> 00:41:51.740
His name's on the tip of my tongue.

00:41:51.740 --> 00:41:53.100
They asked him for a briefing.

00:41:54.060 --> 00:41:58.700
And he said, this division is here on the border

00:41:58.700 --> 00:42:01.180
and elements of this core are over here

00:42:01.180 --> 00:42:02.940
and this division is moving there.

00:42:02.940 --> 00:42:05.260
And we've got these tanks are here

00:42:05.260 --> 00:42:06.700
and these tanks are there.

00:42:08.300 --> 00:42:12.380
And he's saying, we're going to move in from the south

00:42:12.380 --> 00:42:14.780
and we're going to move in from the west

00:42:14.780 --> 00:42:17.020
and we have ships offshore.

00:42:17.020 --> 00:42:19.420
And then he says, if all goes as planned,

00:42:19.420 --> 00:42:21.260
we can be in Tehran by August.

00:42:22.220 --> 00:42:25.020
And I'm sitting there like writing it all down.

00:42:25.020 --> 00:42:26.220
Tehran, Tehran.

00:42:26.220 --> 00:42:30.060
George reaches forward and he turns off his microphone

00:42:30.620 --> 00:42:34.300
and he turns around and he says, did he say Tehran

00:42:34.300 --> 00:42:35.900
or did he say Baghdad?

00:42:37.180 --> 00:42:38.780
Yeah, because we have masses.

00:42:38.780 --> 00:42:40.300
There's a massive difference.

00:42:40.300 --> 00:42:41.980
It's a whole country difference.

00:42:41.980 --> 00:42:45.020
I said, he said Tehran and he says to me,

00:42:45.580 --> 00:42:47.820
have these people lost their fucking minds?

00:42:48.700 --> 00:42:49.500
Wow.

00:42:49.500 --> 00:42:52.380
And then he turns around and he turns the microphone back on

00:42:52.380 --> 00:42:53.340
and he just sits there.

00:42:54.300 --> 00:42:56.860
So I went back to the deputy director's office

00:42:56.860 --> 00:42:58.220
and he said, how'd the meeting go?

00:42:58.780 --> 00:43:02.220
And I said, did you know that we were going to attack Iran?

00:43:03.180 --> 00:43:07.020
And he said, he goes, these idiots have been talking

00:43:07.020 --> 00:43:08.140
about it for a few months.

00:43:08.700 --> 00:43:11.020
I can't imagine it actually happens though.

00:43:11.820 --> 00:43:15.260
And the only reason it didn't was because we got bogged down

00:43:15.260 --> 00:43:16.140
in southern Iraq.

00:43:17.100 --> 00:43:17.500
Yeah.

00:43:18.140 --> 00:43:22.300
When it comes to Condoleezza Rice and again, General Powell,

00:43:22.300 --> 00:43:24.940
were you surprised with the flippant nature?

00:43:25.500 --> 00:43:28.460
Obviously, you know, Condi, she's just bureaucrat at the end

00:43:28.460 --> 00:43:28.940
of the day.

00:43:28.940 --> 00:43:30.860
But with General Powell, he's seen more.

00:43:30.860 --> 00:43:32.460
He knows how difficult these things are.

00:43:32.460 --> 00:43:34.460
He's not the worst sport in many ways.

00:43:35.020 --> 00:43:37.580
Were you surprised by his cavalier attitude?

00:43:37.580 --> 00:43:38.620
Everybody was.

00:43:38.620 --> 00:43:42.220
And he has said in his memoirs and in interviews

00:43:42.220 --> 00:43:45.820
that this is the thing that he is the most ashamed of

00:43:46.140 --> 00:43:48.060
in his life that he should.

00:43:48.060 --> 00:43:50.620
But why did he why did he do this then?

00:43:50.620 --> 00:43:53.340
Was there was was Cheney's power that deep?

00:43:53.340 --> 00:43:56.380
Was it I mean, why was his back against the wall somehow?

00:43:57.260 --> 00:44:03.500
Don't forget that Colin Powell went from Lieutenant Colonel

00:44:03.500 --> 00:44:07.020
to four star general in four years.

00:44:08.060 --> 00:44:10.940
He was in way over his head.

00:44:11.580 --> 00:44:12.220
Wow.

00:44:12.220 --> 00:44:15.020
Don't forget that Condi Rice was the US

00:44:15.020 --> 00:44:18.380
government's leading expert on the Soviet Union.

00:44:18.380 --> 00:44:19.340
Well, guess what?

00:44:19.340 --> 00:44:21.660
The Soviet Union doesn't exist anymore.

00:44:21.660 --> 00:44:24.220
And she didn't know the first friggin thing about Iraq.

00:44:25.500 --> 00:44:27.660
And everybody was afraid of Dick Cheney.

00:44:29.020 --> 00:44:29.740
Wow.

00:44:29.740 --> 00:44:31.020
So you're sitting there.

00:44:31.020 --> 00:44:32.460
The meeting is happening.

00:44:32.460 --> 00:44:35.500
Even by George Tenet standards, they're a little nuts.

00:44:35.500 --> 00:44:37.180
And I don't think the guy would be accused of being

00:44:37.180 --> 00:44:38.300
a peacenik anytime soon.

00:44:38.300 --> 00:44:38.940
No way.

00:44:39.500 --> 00:44:40.700
So let's just go from there.

00:44:40.700 --> 00:44:42.300
Then you understand the plan.

00:44:42.300 --> 00:44:44.780
You're like these folks, they are definitely aggressive.

00:44:44.780 --> 00:44:47.980
They're out of their minds, but they are motivated.

00:44:47.980 --> 00:44:50.540
And they will do what they say they'll do.

00:44:51.900 --> 00:44:53.100
What happens from here?

00:44:53.100 --> 00:44:54.300
Iraq occurs.

00:44:54.300 --> 00:44:57.260
We all know the horrific events.

00:44:57.260 --> 00:44:59.820
But perhaps just kind of talk a little bit about your

00:44:59.820 --> 00:45:02.620
experience watching that and being part of that.

00:45:02.620 --> 00:45:07.740
And then let's get to the crux of your morality,

00:45:07.740 --> 00:45:09.740
which is literally standing up against torture.

00:45:09.740 --> 00:45:13.180
Well, as busy as I was with Iraq, I maintained

00:45:13.180 --> 00:45:14.540
this interest in al-Qaeda.

00:45:15.420 --> 00:45:18.140
To me, this was the war we were supposed to be fighting.

00:45:19.500 --> 00:45:23.740
They had killed 3,000 Americans and were planning attacks

00:45:23.740 --> 00:45:28.860
in bin Laden's words, attacks that would dwarf 9-11.

00:45:29.740 --> 00:45:32.300
And so we had to take this very seriously.

00:45:32.300 --> 00:45:34.540
In the meantime, the policymakers are saying,

00:45:34.540 --> 00:45:37.900
no, we're going to be over here overthrowing Saddam Hussein.

00:45:38.300 --> 00:45:45.340
So I'm watching these special channel cables come in on torture.

00:45:46.060 --> 00:45:51.660
And then I'm saying this is wrong, wrong, wrong.

00:45:51.660 --> 00:45:55.500
There's just, I'm going to get on my soapbox.

00:45:56.220 --> 00:45:59.340
We're supposed to be a nation of laws.

00:45:59.900 --> 00:46:03.100
We're supposed to be a nation governed by the rule of law.

00:46:03.100 --> 00:46:06.940
We're supposed to be a shining beacon of hope

00:46:06.940 --> 00:46:10.620
for human rights and civil rights and civil liberties.

00:46:10.620 --> 00:46:13.340
Every year, by congressional mandate,

00:46:14.540 --> 00:46:18.140
we have to write a human rights report on every country in the world

00:46:18.140 --> 00:46:20.060
with which we have diplomatic relations.

00:46:21.180 --> 00:46:27.740
And then we're on those countries every year to improve their human rights.

00:46:29.180 --> 00:46:34.940
Well, what right do we have when we have a torture program?

00:46:35.500 --> 00:46:38.460
We have an archipelago of secret prisons all over the world

00:46:38.460 --> 00:46:43.420
that even the leaders of those countries didn't know existed in their own country

00:46:43.420 --> 00:46:47.500
because these were private handshake deals between George Tenet

00:46:47.500 --> 00:46:49.820
and the leaders of those intelligence services.

00:46:51.740 --> 00:46:55.900
What kind of country are we where we kidnap people?

00:46:55.900 --> 00:47:01.500
We snatch them off the streets and then send them to third countries to be tortured.

00:47:02.300 --> 00:47:04.140
I was all for rendition.

00:47:04.220 --> 00:47:10.860
A rendition is, let's say you're from Tunisia and I catch you in Pakistan

00:47:11.420 --> 00:47:14.780
and you don't have a passport and you don't have any plausible explanation

00:47:14.780 --> 00:47:16.220
for what you're doing in Pakistan.

00:47:16.780 --> 00:47:19.740
And I send you back to Tunisia because that's your country.

00:47:19.740 --> 00:47:20.620
That's a rendition.

00:47:22.540 --> 00:47:25.820
An extraordinary rendition is something entirely different.

00:47:26.620 --> 00:47:30.060
Let's say, again, you're a Tunisian and I catch you in Pakistan

00:47:30.060 --> 00:47:33.100
with no passport and no plausible explanation for what you're doing there.

00:47:34.060 --> 00:47:39.980
But I send you to Egypt or Syria.

00:47:41.260 --> 00:47:44.700
And we tell the Egyptian or Syrian intelligence services,

00:47:44.700 --> 00:47:47.180
why don't you tenderize this guy for a little bit?

00:47:47.180 --> 00:47:48.540
Let us know what he has to say.

00:47:49.580 --> 00:47:53.180
And then over the course of the next year or 18 months,

00:47:54.220 --> 00:47:56.140
they pull your fingernails out.

00:47:57.660 --> 00:48:00.060
They'll remove one of your testicles.

00:48:00.060 --> 00:48:02.860
Maybe they'll poke an eyeball out.

00:48:03.820 --> 00:48:05.100
They'll electrocute you.

00:48:05.740 --> 00:48:08.460
And then they write up everything that you said and they send it to us

00:48:08.460 --> 00:48:09.900
and they say, here it is.

00:48:09.900 --> 00:48:12.620
And then maybe they let you go,

00:48:12.620 --> 00:48:17.740
but they can't risk you telling people that you were in Syria or Egypt.

00:48:17.740 --> 00:48:20.380
And so you just disappear.

00:48:21.500 --> 00:48:23.820
I want to hear your thoughts on how valid the,

00:48:24.860 --> 00:48:27.980
I suppose, confessions are or whatever here in a second

00:48:27.980 --> 00:48:31.020
because of course, we know that torture isn't necessarily the best way

00:48:31.020 --> 00:48:32.540
of getting the truth out of folks.

00:48:32.540 --> 00:48:35.340
But you're just, let's just go to you in the office.

00:48:35.340 --> 00:48:39.340
You're getting these printouts of the techniques that are being used.

00:48:39.340 --> 00:48:41.660
Can you just tell us a little bit like, what does that look like?

00:48:41.660 --> 00:48:43.420
What are the sentences being said?

00:48:43.420 --> 00:48:46.220
Is it in graphic detail or do they?

00:48:46.220 --> 00:48:47.020
It depends.

00:48:47.020 --> 00:48:48.780
It depends who's writing it.

00:48:48.780 --> 00:48:51.820
Now, there were two notorious contractors at the CIA

00:48:51.820 --> 00:48:53.500
by the names of Mitchell and Jessen.

00:48:54.380 --> 00:49:00.140
And their reporting back was sociopathic.

00:49:00.860 --> 00:49:03.340
It was so clinical and so dry.

00:49:03.340 --> 00:49:04.780
It's like, everything's fine.

00:49:04.780 --> 00:49:08.780
We employed the waterboarding technique and he opened up

00:49:08.780 --> 00:49:11.500
and he gave us all this information.

00:49:11.500 --> 00:49:12.300
No, he didn't.

00:49:13.020 --> 00:49:14.220
You drowned him.

00:49:14.220 --> 00:49:16.940
He had to be revived with the use of CPR.

00:49:17.740 --> 00:49:19.980
And then he stopped talking for six months.

00:49:20.700 --> 00:49:22.700
And all the stuff that you reported in the cable,

00:49:22.700 --> 00:49:27.660
you stole from the FBI officer who actually got him to start talking

00:49:27.660 --> 00:49:31.660
because he offered him a bowl of dates, seriously.

00:49:33.580 --> 00:49:35.740
But then there were other people writing cables,

00:49:35.740 --> 00:49:40.460
like some of the actual CIA employees who were psychologists

00:49:40.460 --> 00:49:43.660
and psychiatrists and nurses and doctors saying,

00:49:43.660 --> 00:49:45.820
whoa, I didn't sign up for this.

00:49:45.820 --> 00:49:48.140
Nobody told me we were going to start torturing him.

00:49:48.140 --> 00:49:49.420
I want to come home.

00:49:49.420 --> 00:49:51.340
Well, that's called a curtailment.

00:49:51.980 --> 00:49:56.940
That's a career ending request to say, I want to come home.

00:49:56.940 --> 00:49:58.220
I don't want to be a part of this.

00:49:58.220 --> 00:50:00.700
You will never get promoted again, ever.

00:50:00.700 --> 00:50:04.220
And so when I saw these curtailment cables coming in,

00:50:05.740 --> 00:50:09.820
I thought to myself, well, somebody's going to say something.

00:50:09.820 --> 00:50:11.580
Somebody's going to blow the whistle on this.

00:50:12.460 --> 00:50:18.220
And I waited and waited and waited and waited for five and a half years.

00:50:18.860 --> 00:50:19.580
Jesus.

00:50:20.220 --> 00:50:26.860
And I resigned from the CIA in 2004 because I got a divorce

00:50:26.860 --> 00:50:31.020
and my sons were young and they moved to Ohio with my ex-wife

00:50:31.020 --> 00:50:34.220
and boys need their dad in their lives.

00:50:34.220 --> 00:50:37.420
And so I decided, if I got to choose between my kids

00:50:37.420 --> 00:50:39.100
and my career, that's an easy decision.

00:50:39.820 --> 00:50:44.540
So I resigned and I took a job with one of the big four

00:50:44.540 --> 00:50:49.340
consulting firms and just waited for somebody to say something.

00:50:50.060 --> 00:50:52.780
Somebody blew the whistle on the secret prisons.

00:50:54.140 --> 00:50:56.060
Somebody blew the whistle on renditions.

00:50:56.060 --> 00:50:58.780
There was even a movie called Rendition in 2005,

00:50:58.780 --> 00:51:01.100
but nobody said anything about torture.

00:51:01.740 --> 00:51:04.380
And how was that for you knowing what was happening,

00:51:04.380 --> 00:51:05.900
knowing, seeing the whitewashing?

00:51:05.900 --> 00:51:07.820
And we can talk, obviously at some point we'll talk,

00:51:07.820 --> 00:51:10.780
we'll get to modern day when it comes to media whitewashing

00:51:10.780 --> 00:51:12.220
and what the U.S. has done overseas.

00:51:13.020 --> 00:51:17.740
That must have made you feel almost like someone who saw a freaking alien.

00:51:17.740 --> 00:51:18.140
Yeah.

00:51:18.140 --> 00:51:19.420
Like someone who was just like,

00:51:19.420 --> 00:51:21.100
believe me, I am telling you the truth,

00:51:21.100 --> 00:51:23.900
but also I would assume you have to keep things quite close to your chest.

00:51:23.900 --> 00:51:24.540
You do.

00:51:24.540 --> 00:51:27.420
That must have torn you apart knowing the truth

00:51:27.420 --> 00:51:32.220
and not seeing one effing responsible journalist tell the truth.

00:51:32.220 --> 00:51:34.540
No, no, not a single one.

00:51:34.540 --> 00:51:37.660
And it came to a head for me in December of 2007.

00:51:39.340 --> 00:51:41.740
Yeah, I actually lost sleep over this.

00:51:41.740 --> 00:51:46.460
And finally, finally, Brian Ross from ABC News called me

00:51:46.460 --> 00:51:50.540
and he said that he had a source who said that I had tortured Abu Zubaydah.

00:51:50.540 --> 00:51:52.940
I said, absolutely untrue.

00:51:52.940 --> 00:51:55.660
I was the only person who was kind to Abu Zubaydah.

00:51:56.220 --> 00:51:59.100
I said, I showed him respect and he showed me respect.

00:51:59.740 --> 00:52:03.100
I was with him for 56 hours after we captured him.

00:52:03.100 --> 00:52:09.340
And I wrote extensive reporting cables on our conversations.

00:52:10.540 --> 00:52:15.820
I said, listen, your source is either sadly mistaken or he's a liar.

00:52:16.620 --> 00:52:19.420
I said, I never laid a hand on Abu Zubaydah or anybody else.

00:52:20.220 --> 00:52:23.020
And he said, and I had no idea this was a reporter's trick,

00:52:23.020 --> 00:52:25.660
but he said, well, you're welcome to come on the show and defend yourself.

00:52:26.940 --> 00:52:28.220
I said, I'll think about it.

00:52:29.260 --> 00:52:32.940
But in the meantime, President Bush had a press conference

00:52:33.580 --> 00:52:38.460
and a reporter said, you know, there are these rumors in the press about a torture program

00:52:38.460 --> 00:52:45.660
and Bush looks right in the camera and he goes, we do not torture like that.

00:52:45.740 --> 00:52:53.100
And I said to my wife, who is a senior CIA officer, I said, he is a bald-faced liar.

00:52:53.100 --> 00:52:56.700
He is looking the American people in the eye and he's just lying to us.

00:52:57.340 --> 00:53:02.300
And then a few days later, he's walking from the South Portico of the White House

00:53:02.300 --> 00:53:04.940
to get onto the helicopter to go to Camp David.

00:53:04.940 --> 00:53:09.900
And a reporter shouts a question about torture and he turns and he shouts,

00:53:09.900 --> 00:53:14.380
well, if there is torture, it's a rogue CIA officer.

00:53:15.340 --> 00:53:16.460
Oh my God.

00:53:16.460 --> 00:53:20.540
And I said to my wife, Brian Ross is sources at the White House

00:53:20.540 --> 00:53:22.140
and they're going to try to pin this on me.

00:53:23.340 --> 00:53:27.260
So I called Brian Ross and I said, bro, if there was a slow zoom,

00:53:27.260 --> 00:53:31.820
if it was the Truman show and you were on camera, it would have been the dun dun dun dun.

00:53:33.020 --> 00:53:33.740
Yep.

00:53:33.740 --> 00:53:36.860
When Truman sees the little thing in the ceiling, that was me.

00:53:37.500 --> 00:53:41.180
So I called Brian Ross and I said, I'll give you your interview.

00:53:41.180 --> 00:53:43.660
And I told my wife in the days leading up to it,

00:53:43.660 --> 00:53:44.540
I said, you know what?

00:53:45.340 --> 00:53:47.900
No matter what he asked me, I'm going to tell the truth.

00:53:49.180 --> 00:53:50.220
And so that's what I did.

00:53:50.860 --> 00:53:52.940
Well, let's talk a little bit about the truth.

00:53:52.940 --> 00:53:54.060
You did the interview.

00:53:54.060 --> 00:53:56.380
Seems as if they were trying to scapegoat you,

00:53:56.380 --> 00:53:58.140
which I would assume happens regularly.

00:53:58.140 --> 00:53:58.380
Yes.

00:53:59.900 --> 00:54:02.860
The night before the interview, what was your thought process?

00:54:03.500 --> 00:54:08.060
Were you preparing, because obviously in these moments,

00:54:08.060 --> 00:54:11.740
when the camera's on, you only have a finite amount of time.

00:54:11.740 --> 00:54:13.500
And you want to get your points across.

00:54:14.540 --> 00:54:16.220
How was that night before?

00:54:16.220 --> 00:54:20.540
And can you just sort of talk a little bit about what you decided to say?

00:54:20.540 --> 00:54:21.260
Yeah.

00:54:21.260 --> 00:54:23.420
I had never been on TV before.

00:54:23.420 --> 00:54:26.220
I had never done a press interview before.

00:54:27.180 --> 00:54:28.700
And I was kind of winging it.

00:54:28.700 --> 00:54:32.380
I was a little bit nervous, but I was determined to tell the truth.

00:54:32.380 --> 00:54:33.260
And I thought, you know what?

00:54:33.260 --> 00:54:34.940
The truth will set you free.

00:54:34.940 --> 00:54:35.260
Right?

00:54:36.140 --> 00:54:36.940
So irony.

00:54:37.660 --> 00:54:39.980
I, irony's right.

00:54:40.540 --> 00:54:45.660
So my wife went with me and sat just off camera.

00:54:46.220 --> 00:54:49.820
And in the interview, I made three points.

00:54:49.820 --> 00:54:52.140
I said the CIA was torturing its prisoners.

00:54:52.700 --> 00:54:56.140
I said that torture was official US government policy.

00:54:56.140 --> 00:54:57.660
It was not the result of a rogue.

00:54:58.380 --> 00:55:02.620
And I said that the policy had been personally approved by the president himself.

00:55:03.900 --> 00:55:07.500
And so at the end of the interview, I said to her, how did I do?

00:55:07.500 --> 00:55:08.380
She said, great.

00:55:09.260 --> 00:55:12.060
I said, I didn't say anything classified, right?

00:55:12.060 --> 00:55:13.900
And she said, no, you did great.

00:55:15.180 --> 00:55:19.100
Fast forward to January of 2020.

00:55:20.220 --> 00:55:24.620
My wife and I are divorced and we're in a custody battle.

00:55:25.420 --> 00:55:29.740
And I was on the stand and my lawyer asked me if she was a good wife.

00:55:29.740 --> 00:55:31.260
I said, she was an amazing wife.

00:55:32.140 --> 00:55:36.460
I said, when I blew the whistle on the torture program, she was so supportive.

00:55:36.460 --> 00:55:39.980
She sat just off camera and I told the story that I just told you.

00:55:40.780 --> 00:55:44.860
Then she gets on the stand and her lawyer says,

00:55:44.860 --> 00:55:49.260
is it true that you went with him to ABC News when he gave the whistleblowing interview?

00:55:49.260 --> 00:55:49.980
She said, yes.

00:55:50.540 --> 00:55:51.340
Why did you do that?

00:55:51.900 --> 00:55:55.660
She said, I was instructed to do it by the CIA's Office of Security.

00:55:55.660 --> 00:55:58.700
And I continued to report back to them on his activities.

00:56:00.780 --> 00:56:01.820
Wow.

00:56:01.820 --> 00:56:02.380
All right.

00:56:02.380 --> 00:56:06.300
So let's talk a little bit post now that there's a spy in the house.

00:56:07.340 --> 00:56:09.900
Again, if you're a check out your husband,

00:56:09.900 --> 00:56:11.980
make sure your husband's wife aren't wearing a wire.

00:56:13.740 --> 00:56:17.020
So that's a total abuse of trust.

00:56:17.020 --> 00:56:18.780
That is so sad and disgusting.

00:56:19.500 --> 00:56:23.740
I can't imagine how that made your skin crawl, to be honest, because that's disgusting.

00:56:25.180 --> 00:56:29.100
But so you do the interview and obviously once again,

00:56:29.100 --> 00:56:30.940
this is another life-changing moment.

00:56:31.580 --> 00:56:35.740
Can you talk about what happened post interview and how long was it before you're like,

00:56:35.740 --> 00:56:38.780
oh my God, the perversion is going to hit the fan.

00:56:38.780 --> 00:56:44.860
So as you might imagine, the very next day, the CIA filed what's called a crimes report against

00:56:44.860 --> 00:56:48.220
me with the FBI saying that I had revealed classified information.

00:56:48.860 --> 00:56:54.220
The FBI investigated me from December of 07 to December of 08.

00:56:54.220 --> 00:56:57.500
And in December of 08, they determined that I had not committed a crime.

00:56:58.620 --> 00:57:00.940
It's illegal to classify a crime.

00:57:01.580 --> 00:57:09.580
So if you've got a program that's illegal, a program where CIA officers are violating U.S. law,

00:57:09.580 --> 00:57:12.460
you can't classify that, right?

00:57:13.420 --> 00:57:18.380
So they sent my attorneys what's called a declination letter, declining to prosecute me.

00:57:18.380 --> 00:57:20.780
And we actually went out to dinner that night and celebrated.

00:57:22.380 --> 00:57:29.260
But three weeks later, Barack Obama is inaugurated as president and he names John Brennan,

00:57:29.260 --> 00:57:33.420
one of my old nemeses, and one of the fathers of the torture program,

00:57:33.420 --> 00:57:37.580
names John Brennan as the deputy national security advisor for counterterrorism.

00:57:37.580 --> 00:57:40.540
And you can watch John Brennan on MSNBC tonight.

00:57:40.540 --> 00:57:46.140
He's on Rachel Maddow talking about God knows what other useless bullshit.

00:57:46.140 --> 00:57:51.740
Yep. I had no idea that John Brennan asked the Justice Department to secretly reopen the

00:57:51.740 --> 00:57:57.340
case against me. And for the next three years, my phones were tapped, my emails were intercepted.

00:57:57.900 --> 00:58:01.420
And your wife was a spy. And my wife was spying on me.

00:58:01.420 --> 00:58:05.020
And teams of FBI agents were following me everywhere I went.

00:58:05.020 --> 00:58:10.380
You were being gangstocked. Did you feel like you were being driven completely and utterly insane?

00:58:10.380 --> 00:58:13.020
I had no idea any of it was happening. Wow.

00:58:13.900 --> 00:58:16.460
I really thought it was over. I really did.

00:58:17.340 --> 00:58:25.420
And so in January of 2012, I was arrested and charged with five felonies,

00:58:25.420 --> 00:58:29.340
including three counts of espionage. I hadn't committed espionage.

00:58:29.340 --> 00:58:35.180
And those charges were eventually dropped, but not before I was forced into a deal.

00:58:35.980 --> 00:58:42.060
And just to make matters worse, in the interim, after I blew the whistle, John Kerry hired me

00:58:42.060 --> 00:58:46.140
to be the chief investigator on the Senate Foreign Relations Committee. This is a major job,

00:58:46.140 --> 00:58:52.140
serious job. And one of the great things about that job is you get to have

00:58:52.940 --> 00:58:58.300
a lunch with foreign diplomats all the time. And I used to love that part of my job overseas

00:58:58.300 --> 00:59:07.180
at the CIA. So I'm invited to lunch one day by a Japanese diplomat. And we go to this place on

00:59:07.180 --> 00:59:12.700
Capitol Hill. And he asks me during the course of the lunch, so what's next for you? And I said,

00:59:13.420 --> 00:59:17.500
I think I'm going to resign soon. I promised Senator Kerry I'd give him two years. It's

00:59:17.500 --> 00:59:22.140
been two and a half. I said, I have five kids. I need to I need to put them through college.

00:59:22.700 --> 00:59:30.860
Yeah. And he goes, no, don't do that. If you give me information, I can give you money.

00:59:32.540 --> 00:59:38.060
Oh, that's so obvious. I said, what's wrong with you? Are you crazy? I said, do you have

00:59:38.060 --> 00:59:44.140
any idea how many times I've made that pitch in my career? Shame on you. And I went directly

00:59:45.100 --> 00:59:50.780
without stopping to the office of the Senate security officer. And I said, I was just pitched by

00:59:50.780 --> 00:59:58.700
a foreign intelligence officer. And he told me to write it up. And he would send it to the FBI.

00:59:58.700 --> 01:00:05.180
So he did. The next day, two FBI agents came to interview me. And they said, look, here's

01:00:05.180 --> 01:00:09.740
what we want you to do. We want you to call them back, invite him to lunch,

01:00:09.820 --> 01:00:16.460
and try to get him to tell you exactly what information he wants and what he's willing to pay for it.

01:00:17.100 --> 01:00:22.220
I said, great. I said, you want me to like wear a wire or something? They said, no,

01:00:22.220 --> 01:00:26.460
we're going to be at the next table. I said, okay, cool. So I invite him to lunch. He says, yes.

01:00:27.500 --> 01:00:31.820
Then that morning they called me and they said that something had come up. They couldn't be

01:00:31.820 --> 01:00:36.060
at the next table, but just write him another memo and tell them what happened.

01:00:36.060 --> 01:00:42.300
So I did it. And then they asked me to do it a third time and a fourth time and a fifth time,

01:00:42.940 --> 01:00:50.140
which I did. And then he told me that he had gotten promoted. He got his dream job. He was

01:00:50.140 --> 01:00:55.900
going to be the number two in the Japanese embassy in Cairo. Nice to know you. I shook his

01:00:55.900 --> 01:01:02.540
hand and I never saw him again. Well, a year later, I've been arrested and

01:01:03.500 --> 01:01:10.860
the Justice Department releases discovery to us. And there were two things that really jumped out at

01:01:10.860 --> 01:01:19.580
us. First was that there never was any Japanese diplomat. He was an FBI agent undercover trying

01:01:19.580 --> 01:01:27.500
to get me to commit actual espionage. But every time I met him, I reported it back to the FBI.

01:01:27.740 --> 01:01:31.740
Hmm. And so finally, one of them wrote a memo and they said,

01:01:31.740 --> 01:01:35.180
clearly he's not going to take the bait. We should just end this operation.

01:01:36.300 --> 01:01:42.540
And so they did. The other thing that we found was a letter from John Brennan

01:01:42.540 --> 01:01:48.860
to Eric Holder saying, charge him with espionage. And Eric Holder wrote back and said,

01:01:48.860 --> 01:01:54.460
my people don't think he committed espionage. And then John Brennan wrote back and said,

01:01:54.460 --> 01:02:01.180
charge him anyway and make him defend himself. Jesus Christ. And they waited until I went bankrupt

01:02:01.180 --> 01:02:06.300
before they dropped the charges. Wow. Of course, when you're going against the federal government,

01:02:07.580 --> 01:02:15.660
it is impossible to outlast them. They have a 98.2% conviction rate, according to ProPublica,

01:02:15.660 --> 01:02:22.380
for that very reason, because they have unlimited resources. You don't and you can't outlast

01:02:22.860 --> 01:02:26.300
them. And it's scary as hell, man. When you're looking at these charges, I mean,

01:02:26.300 --> 01:02:31.260
assume I'm not sure what was what was the plea deal or what was the arrangement that you all

01:02:31.260 --> 01:02:39.100
ended up making? Well, at first, they offered me 45 years. And one of the US attorney, one of

01:02:39.100 --> 01:02:42.540
the assistant US attorneys. Dude, did you throw up? I would have thrown up. I seriously would

01:02:42.540 --> 01:02:45.740
have thrown up and I would have been like, I'm just I'm like, it's like South Park.

01:02:46.380 --> 01:02:51.980
It was a full time job among family and friends to

01:02:54.300 --> 01:03:00.860
not allow me to commit suicide. Wow. Yeah. I mean, people were people.

01:03:02.220 --> 01:03:09.100
My sister came down and stayed up all night long just so I didn't go downstairs to the garage and

01:03:09.100 --> 01:03:14.220
put an exhaust pipe in my mouth. And you were really, I mean, it was it was that tense.

01:03:14.220 --> 01:03:20.620
Oh, yeah, I was good. I was going to do it. Yeah. Oh, man. Yeah. So they went.

01:03:21.820 --> 01:03:26.780
They started at 45 and this this woman said, take a plea, Mr. Kiriakou,

01:03:26.780 --> 01:03:32.700
and you might live to meet your grandchildren. Oh, my God. That's what she told me.

01:03:33.980 --> 01:03:40.140
So I put on my bravest face and I said, I'm not doing 45 minutes. I told her.

01:03:41.020 --> 01:03:45.900
So they let a little bit, a little bit of time pass. And then they came back with 10 years.

01:03:45.900 --> 01:03:49.580
Take a plea to an espionage charge and do 10. That was on a Monday.

01:03:50.300 --> 01:03:55.180
On Wednesday, they came back with eight. And then on Friday, they came back with five.

01:03:56.140 --> 01:04:00.780
Now, my lead attorney was this legendary criminal defense attorney, Plato Kacheris.

01:04:01.580 --> 01:04:07.100
He's a he was a giant in Washington. And he said to me, you know, he said,

01:04:07.100 --> 01:04:12.860
I've been an attorney here for 52 years. And I've never seen them come down in time.

01:04:13.580 --> 01:04:18.460
Usually they make an offer 10 years. And if you say no, the next offers for 15 years,

01:04:19.020 --> 01:04:25.900
and then 20 years, right? I said, why are they coming down in time? And he said,

01:04:25.900 --> 01:04:30.060
because they have a shit case and they know it's shit, we're going to trial, he says.

01:04:31.020 --> 01:04:34.460
So I said, okay, well, then they came back with three and a half.

01:04:35.500 --> 01:04:39.180
And that was worth countering. So we countered with a year.

01:04:40.220 --> 01:04:45.260
And they said they would go two and a half, but that was best and final.

01:04:46.620 --> 01:04:51.020
So I had 24 hours to consider it. And my wife and I stayed up all night long.

01:04:51.020 --> 01:04:55.820
And I decided to turn it down. I said, I haven't done anything wrong.

01:04:56.780 --> 01:05:00.940
And as soon as I get in front of a jury, they're going to realize how ridiculous this is.

01:05:00.940 --> 01:05:05.020
I'm surprised you were able to be in the CIA with those big brass balls of yours clinging

01:05:05.020 --> 01:05:08.060
all around the hallway. It's a horrible thing to have for a spy.

01:05:09.820 --> 01:05:13.580
So I emailed them at six in the morning and I said, I've been up all night.

01:05:14.220 --> 01:05:17.420
I've read all the literature. I decided I'm turning it down.

01:05:18.220 --> 01:05:21.820
So Plato emails me and he says, these were his exact words.

01:05:21.820 --> 01:05:24.300
You stupid son of a bitch, take the deal.

01:05:26.140 --> 01:05:29.260
And then two other lawyers weighed in and one said,

01:05:29.900 --> 01:05:32.220
we're on our way to your house, put on a pot of coffee.

01:05:33.020 --> 01:05:37.340
So these were the two guys that I liked and, and respected the most.

01:05:37.340 --> 01:05:42.460
I had 11 attorneys on this team. And I mean, these are major players.

01:05:42.460 --> 01:05:46.220
This was Aiken, Gump and Strauss, one of the biggest law firms in the world.

01:05:46.220 --> 01:05:51.980
The head of their white collar defense practice was one of my three leads.

01:05:52.540 --> 01:05:55.820
So what was it about your case specifically that they were so drawn to?

01:05:55.820 --> 01:05:58.620
They believed I had been wronged. They came right out and said it.

01:05:59.260 --> 01:06:00.940
This was a political case.

01:06:00.940 --> 01:06:03.500
This was because I blew the whistle on the torture program.

01:06:03.500 --> 01:06:04.540
Right. It's Brennan's.

01:06:05.100 --> 01:06:05.820
Yeah. Brennan's.

01:06:05.820 --> 01:06:06.460
His nature.

01:06:06.460 --> 01:06:06.700
Yeah.

01:06:06.700 --> 01:06:07.180
Yeah.

01:06:07.180 --> 01:06:08.620
Honestly. Oh my God.

01:06:08.620 --> 01:06:13.260
So one of them pulled me aside and said, listen, he said,

01:06:13.260 --> 01:06:18.860
if you were my own brother, I would beg you to take this deal.

01:06:19.820 --> 01:06:21.580
He said, you know what your problem is?

01:06:21.580 --> 01:06:24.700
And this is what really, this is what did it to me.

01:06:24.700 --> 01:06:26.700
He said, you know what your problem is?

01:06:26.700 --> 01:06:31.420
Your problem is you think this is about justice and it's not about justice.

01:06:31.420 --> 01:06:34.940
It's about mitigating damage. Take the deal.

01:06:36.300 --> 01:06:36.620
Wow.

01:06:37.260 --> 01:06:41.660
So I took the deal and I ended up doing 23 months.

01:06:42.460 --> 01:06:45.020
And so let's talk a little bit about that experience.

01:06:46.300 --> 01:06:49.260
I assume you turned yourself in or you weren't, you weren't.

01:06:49.260 --> 01:06:53.580
Well, at sentencing, you know, the judge took a swipe at me and she said,

01:06:53.580 --> 01:06:59.100
Mr. Kariaku, I wish that I could give you the maximum because that's what I would give you.

01:06:59.100 --> 01:07:02.140
Well, you know what? She could have given me the maximum,

01:07:02.140 --> 01:07:06.700
but every reporter, every national security reporter in Washington is a raid in the gallery

01:07:06.700 --> 01:07:13.260
there. And she's trying to look like, you know, the big defender of the intelligence community.

01:07:14.060 --> 01:07:15.260
Not her job, by the way.

01:07:15.260 --> 01:07:19.580
Yeah, not her job, exactly. But she's a former prosecutor, just like all the other judges are.

01:07:20.860 --> 01:07:27.420
So my attorneys asked her to send me to a minimum security work camp.

01:07:28.300 --> 01:07:31.740
No bars on the windows, no fences, the doors unlocked.

01:07:31.740 --> 01:07:33.660
You're free to come and go as you please.

01:07:33.660 --> 01:07:40.140
You're just on your honor not to abscond. And so the prosecutors had no objection.

01:07:40.140 --> 01:07:45.500
So she ordered that I be sent to the federal work camp at Loretto, Pennsylvania,

01:07:45.500 --> 01:07:53.020
in central Pennsylvania, the mountains. So I drive up there. It's crazy. You just,

01:07:53.020 --> 01:07:56.620
you knock on the door and you say, I'm here to turn myself in.

01:07:57.340 --> 01:08:00.860
So I went to the camp and I knocked on the door. I said, I'm John Kariaku.

01:08:00.860 --> 01:08:07.180
I'm supposed to turn myself in. And the guy says, you need to go across the street to the prison.

01:08:07.180 --> 01:08:10.780
They'll process you there. And then they'll walk you back over here. I said, okay.

01:08:12.300 --> 01:08:18.140
So everybody that, you know, we had a caravan. There was like a documentary film crew and two

01:08:18.140 --> 01:08:24.460
of my lawyers and my cousin and his son. And so they all drive away. I go to the prison

01:08:24.540 --> 01:08:31.740
and, you know, they pat me down and they put me through the metal detector. And then

01:08:33.100 --> 01:08:39.340
the guy cuffs me and he starts taking me around to the back of the prison.

01:08:39.340 --> 01:08:44.860
And I said, no, no, no. I'm supposed to be at the camp across the street. And he said,

01:08:45.900 --> 01:08:51.740
not according to my paperwork, you're not. And I was like, oh my God, I said, take it easy.

01:08:51.740 --> 01:08:56.780
There's nothing you can do. If you, if you, you know, put up a fight, they're going to put

01:08:56.780 --> 01:09:04.140
you in solitary. So don't say anything. So I didn't. It took me five days to get access to a phone.

01:09:04.780 --> 01:09:10.060
What was that like when he put the cuffs on you? What was that feeling like? Did you panic and alone?

01:09:10.060 --> 01:09:16.540
Yeah. Yeah. Panic. Because nobody had any idea where I was. Everybody thought I was, you

01:09:16.540 --> 01:09:22.300
know, at the camp, I'm working at the university in town, you know, sweeping the library or whatever.

01:09:22.940 --> 01:09:29.500
Oh no, I'm not. So I called my lawyers and I said, hey, they put me in the actual prison

01:09:29.500 --> 01:09:35.900
with the pedophiles and the drug king pins and the mafia dons. What do I do? And he said, oh my

01:09:35.900 --> 01:09:40.540
God, he said, well, we could file a motion, but it'll be two years before we get a hearing

01:09:40.540 --> 01:09:44.380
and you'll be home by then. He said, buddy, I'm sorry, you're going to have to tough it

01:09:44.380 --> 01:09:53.820
out. Oh my God. Well, it's the wrong jail. Yeah. So I decided that moment that I was trained for this.

01:09:54.700 --> 01:10:01.340
I had lived in far worse places than Loretto, Pennsylvania. And that I was going to use my

01:10:01.340 --> 01:10:07.980
CIA training to make sure that I stayed safe and at the very top of the prison social heap.

01:10:08.540 --> 01:10:12.860
And that's what I did. And can you explain a little bit? Because one thing to say it,

01:10:12.860 --> 01:10:17.500
another thing to do it. What techniques did you use to survive?

01:10:17.500 --> 01:10:21.980
Well, this is the subject of my second book. I called it Doing Time Like a Spy,

01:10:22.700 --> 01:10:29.420
How the CIA Taught Me to Survive and Thrive in Prison. And I start the book off with 20 life lessons

01:10:29.420 --> 01:10:33.820
that the CIA taught me. And some of them were kind of tongue in cheek, like admit nothing,

01:10:33.820 --> 01:10:39.900
deny everything, make counter accusations. But some were serious, like let others do your dirty

01:10:39.900 --> 01:10:48.380
work. And I'll give you an example. There was a guy, I had an empty bed in my cubicle in my cell.

01:10:49.100 --> 01:10:57.740
And this guy wanted to move into the cell. Well, I didn't want to, we didn't allow

01:10:57.740 --> 01:11:03.900
pedophiles in our cell, right? No pedophiles was my rule. Of course. So I said to him,

01:11:04.780 --> 01:11:09.900
we used to call him cat in the hat, because he had a really weird, like elongated head,

01:11:09.900 --> 01:11:17.420
like a birth defect, right? Okay. So I said, well, what's your crime? He said, murder for hire.

01:11:17.420 --> 01:11:26.700
And I said, ooh, that doesn't sound so great. I said, explain yourself. And he said that

01:11:27.660 --> 01:11:35.660
he had a lot of gambling debts. He owed the mafia in Pittsburgh, $100,000 in gambling debts.

01:11:36.300 --> 01:11:42.780
And he took out a life insurance policy on his business partner. He hired a hit man. The guy

01:11:42.780 --> 01:11:49.260
flew in from New Orleans and killed his business partner and then flew back to New Orleans.

01:11:50.220 --> 01:11:53.820
But the first person the cops are going to look at is,

01:11:54.460 --> 01:11:59.500
who is it that has a life insurance policy on the guy that's been murdered? Always.

01:11:59.500 --> 01:12:05.820
Always. So of course, three days later, they arrest him and he immediately rats out the hit man.

01:12:05.820 --> 01:12:09.420
And I said, oh, wait a minute. So not only are you a murderer, but you're a rat.

01:12:10.220 --> 01:12:15.580
And rats have to sit at the pedophile table in the cafeteria. So I don't want any rat in my

01:12:15.580 --> 01:12:23.980
room. So forget it. Well, he didn't like me, not even a little bit. Okay. So put that aside.

01:12:24.860 --> 01:12:30.140
There was another guy in there that went by the nickname truck because he had been a long-distance

01:12:30.140 --> 01:12:39.740
truck driver. Okay. Truck was a very, very dangerous guy. He would just, like he had

01:12:39.740 --> 01:12:45.100
pedophiles in his cell and they'd be sleeping and he would just start beating the shit out of

01:12:45.100 --> 01:12:50.540
them while they were sleeping, you know, just because. Right, right. So

01:12:51.420 --> 01:12:57.100
truck for whatever reason desperately sought my approval. He claimed he had been in the CIA

01:12:57.100 --> 01:13:01.420
and it was all just nonsense he had seen from movies that he was just repeating back to me.

01:13:02.620 --> 01:13:05.660
But the reason I didn't like this guy is in the 70s,

01:13:07.100 --> 01:13:12.460
on his long-distance truck route, truck stop prostitutes began turning up dead.

01:13:12.940 --> 01:13:19.020
Right. Strangled. All of them just found in the bush on the side of the highway.

01:13:20.780 --> 01:13:27.500
One day truck picks up a prostitute who happened to be 16 years old and he had sex with her and

01:13:27.500 --> 01:13:33.660
he strangled her and threw her out of the truck and she lived and she was able to identify him.

01:13:33.660 --> 01:13:38.700
Well, the cops knew that he was a serial killer. They knew that he had been the one

01:13:38.700 --> 01:13:42.540
murdering these prostitutes and there were 14 of them that he had killed.

01:13:43.180 --> 01:13:47.340
But these were the days before DNA and so they just couldn't prove it.

01:13:47.340 --> 01:13:53.100
So they got him on the kidnapping charge and they sentenced him to 20 years and he did the whole

01:13:53.100 --> 01:13:59.180
20 and he got out in the 90s. But the cops had it out for him because they knew he was the

01:13:59.180 --> 01:14:08.540
serial killer. So they worked with his parole officer to harass him, to just wake him up all

01:14:08.540 --> 01:14:13.820
hours of the day and night, search his house, which you have to submit to because you're on

01:14:13.820 --> 01:14:21.340
parole. Well, sure enough, they found a gun in his house and in response, he beat up the

